// Batch 10
D6("tns",["The Type 'n Speak is Blazie Engineering's typewriter-keyboard sibling of the Braille 'n Speak. Deane Blazie said it was built for people who liked the Braille 'n Speak but did not know braille. The manual calls it a laptop-style computer with a built-in speech synthesizer that speaks every prompt and everything you type.","Physically it is a flat box about a foot wide, seven inches from front to back and one inch high, weighing about two pounds with its internal rechargeable battery. It has no display screen at all. The whole top surface is a laptop-style QWERTY keyboard.","Set it down with the spacebar nearest you. About halfway back along the right edge is the on/off rocker switch. Rock it away from you to turn the unit on and toward you to turn it off.","Many keys are renamed for the Type 'n Speak. The top-right key (Break on a PC) is Help, and Shift+Help enters Learn mode, which names each key without acting on it. Down the right side, Home becomes Top, PgUp and PgDn jump by paragraph, and End becomes Bottom. The arrow keys sit in the bottom-right corner.","The manual says it stores about 300 print pages in RAM (758 KB) and about 800 more in 2 MB of flash memory. It serves as a notebook, address book, calendar, calculator, stopwatch and timer."],
{ram:"758 KB (Type 'n Speak manual; earlier record said 768 KB)",wt:"About 2 lb (manual)",dim:"About 12 × 7 × 1 in (manual)"},
["https://support.freedomscientific.com/Content/Documents/Manuals/Legacy/Type_N_SpeakDoc/Type_N_Speak_UserMan.txt","https://afb.org/aw/1/1/16189"]);
D6("vnclassic",["The VoiceNote is a BrailleNote without the braille display. Pulse Data and HumanWare introduced it at Closing the Gap 2000, with either an eight-dot braille keyboard (BT) or a QWERTY keyboard (QT). It runs the same Windows CE-based KeySoft suite as the BrailleNote, but output is speech only.","AccessWorld measured it at 9.5 × 5 × 1 inches and 1 pound 10 ounces, the same footprint as the BrailleNote. The case is gray. On the BT model the nine keys (eight braille dots plus a space bar) are green and arranged on a gentle curve for comfort.","Around the case are a PC Card slot, serial and parallel ports, a modem jack, an AC adapter jack, an earphone jack, a CompactFlash slot and an infrared port. Power comes from a rechargeable nickel-metal-hydride battery.","User reports on the BrailleNote list say early BT keys were fairly noisy and clacky and later units were quieter. QT owners described their keyboards as very quiet.","AccessWorld priced it at $2,295. Its strengths were the KeyWord word processor and KeyPlan calendar. You cannot install ordinary Windows programs on it, and AccessWorld found the e-mail and GPS (Atlas Speaks-based) features needed improvement."],
{price:"$2,295 (AccessWorld 2003)",wt:"1 lb 10 oz",dim:"9.5 × 5 × 1 in",io:"PC Card slot, serial, parallel, modem, earphone jack, CompactFlash, infrared",bat:"Rechargeable NiMH"},
["https://afb.org/aw/4/3/14847","https://www.mail-archive.com/braillenote@list.pulsedata.com/msg24873.html"]);
D6("vnmp",["The VoiceNote mPower is the speech-only member of HumanWare's BrailleNote mPower family, announced June 28, 2005. It has a braille (BT) or QWERTY (QT) keyboard and no braille display.","AccessWorld says the mPower family is the same size as earlier BrailleNotes and VoiceNotes, with the same keyboard and thumb keys. The gray case gave way to a silver one, and the teal keys to forest green.","Along the back panel are the power jack, phone line jack and serial port, a CompactFlash slot, an SD slot, two USB host ports and one USB client port. The PC Card slot stays on the right side. On the left are the earphone jack, power switch, an external-microphone jack and a recessed Record button, and there is also an internal microphone. The parallel port was dropped.","Inside are 128 MB of flash storage, Bluetooth and an internal 56K modem. Headphones give good stereo sound, but the built-in speaker is the same modest one as before. AccessWorld noted shorter battery life than earlier units and no DAISY or Audible playback at launch.","The VoiceNote mPower BT or QT cost $1,995 plus $45 shipping. Owners of older units could have their hardware moved into a new mPower case through HumanWare's 'transplant' option."],
{price:"$1,995 plus $45 shipping (2005)",io:"2 USB host, 1 USB client, SD, CompactFlash, PC Card, serial, modem, earphone and microphone jacks, Bluetooth"},
["https://afb.org/aw/7/1/14531"]);
D6("vnapex",["The VoiceNote Apex is the speech-only Apex, introduced in July 2010. It is physically the same as the BrailleNote Apex but has no braille display, with an eight-dot braille keyboard (BT) or a QWERTY keyboard (QT).","A dealer spec sheet gives 612 g (1.35 lb) and 244 × 143 × 19 mm (9.61 × 5.63 × 0.78 in), a thinner case than the older VoiceNotes.","It has stereo speakers, an omnidirectional internal microphone, a user-replaceable lithium-ion battery and jacks for an external speaker or headphones and a microphone. A real-time clock keeps time for 30 days on backup power.","Ports include one SD/SDHC slot, a 10/100 Ethernet port, a USB 2.0 client port for ActiveSync and three USB 2.0 host ports. Wi-Fi (802.11b/g) and Bluetooth 2.1 are built in. A USB voice modem and a Bluetooth GPS receiver were optional.","Inside are a Freescale i.MX31 processor, 256 MB of SDRAM and 8 GB of storage, running Windows CE 6 with KeySoft. A braille terminal mode lets it work with a computer's screen reader."],
{wt:"612 g (1.35 lb)",dim:"244 × 143 × 19 mm (9.61 × 5.63 × 0.78 in)",io:"SD/SDHC, Ethernet, USB client, 3 USB host, Wi-Fi b/g, Bluetooth 2.1",bat:"User-replaceable lithium-ion"},
["https://editmicro.co.za/product/voicenote-apex-bt/"]);
D6("bookport",["The Book Port is the American Printing House for the Blind's first handheld e-book and audio reader, released in 2003. It reads text, HTML, BRF, Word, RTF and DAISY 2 and 3 books aloud with a DoubleTalk synthesizer, plays MP3 and WAV files and records voice memos.","AccessWorld measured it at 4 × 2.25 × 1 inches and 6 ounces with batteries. APH's own manual gives 2.75 × 4.75 × 1 inches. The case is black and the keys are labeled in yellow for high contrast.","The face holds 18 round keys, evenly spaced. The top 12 are a telephone keypad with a nib on 5. Below them are two rows of three, A, B, C and D, E, F, which set speed and volume and control recording.","On the top edge are the headphone jack and USB port. The memory-card slot is on the right side, and the battery compartment (two AA batteries) and a belt clip are on the back. There is no power switch and no built-in speaker: press 2 to read, press it again to stop, and the unit turns itself off. You must use headphones or an external speaker.","The number keys navigate. 1 and 3 move by sentence, 4 and 6 by paragraph, 7 and 9 by page, 8 gives file statistics and 0 switches to the settings keypad. APH later discontinued it. One reviewer still preferred it to the Victor Reader Stream for its braille-style text input and markup of Audible books."],
{dim:"4 × 2.25 × 1 in (AccessWorld); 2.75 × 4.75 × 1 in (APH manual)",wt:"6 oz with batteries"},
["https://www.afb.org/aw/5/4/14712","https://afb.org/aw/5/4/14711","https://tech.aph.org/bp_doc.htm","https://afb.org/aw/9/5/14232"]);
D6("bookportplus",["The Book Port Plus is APH's 2010 successor to the Book Port: a pocket DAISY player, text reader, MP3 player and recorder with Wi-Fi for internet radio, podcast downloads and wireless file transfer.","A product listing gives 4.4 by 2.2 inches and 3.9 ounces, smaller and lighter than the original Book Port. It runs on a rechargeable lithium-polymer battery, charged by AC adapter or USB.","In the normal position the speaker grille is farthest from you, and the telephone keypad, with a very pronounced dot on 5, is closest. Above the keypad are arrow keys, a Menu key, a Bookmark key, a Go To key, a Title key, a Play/Stop key and a Record key.","The top edge holds the microphone and earphone jacks, the right edge the volume control and key lock, the bottom edge the AC jack and USB port, and the left edge the SD card slot (SDHC up to 32 GB). The battery door is at the bottom of the back. It has a built-in speaker and microphone.","It plays NLS, RFB&D, Bookshare and Audible books (once the unit is authorized) and reads Word, text and HTML files with built-in text-to-speech. Earbuds, two USB cables and a carrying pouch came in the box."],
{wt:"3.9 oz",dim:"4.4 × 2.2 in (listed depth is a typo)",bat:"Rechargeable lithium-polymer (AC or USB)",io:"Wi-Fi, SD/SDHC up to 32 GB, USB, earphone and microphone jacks"},
["https://tech.aph.org/bt_doc.htm","https://www.assistive-tech.net/products/116138"]);
D6("bksense",["The BookSense is HIMS's pocket book and audio player, sold in the US by GW Micro from 2009. It reads DAISY books (NLS, Bookshare, Audible), text and Word files, plays music and records. The standard model cost $349.","AccessWorld compares it to a candy-bar cell phone, 4.25 × 1.85 × 0.73 inches and 4 ounces, the smallest of the players it tested. A lanyard attaches at the top center.","The front, from the top: stereo speakers, a raised date-and-time button, a five-way control with nibs on the four arrows and a small square Menu button in the center, then a 3-by-4 phone keypad. The keypad has a nib on 5, and alternate keys are slightly concave or convex.","On the right side are the Play/Pause button (hold it for power), a Record button and a key-lock switch. On the left side are the Mode button, volume up and down and the SD card slot. The top edge has the microphone and 3.5 mm headphone jacks, the bottom the AC jack and USB port, and the back the battery compartment.","It reads with NeoSpeech Paul and Kate voices. A user report gives about 12 hours per charge and 2.5 hours to recharge. Holding the clock button turns on voice commands."],
{price:"$349 (standard)",wt:"4 oz",dim:"4.25 × 1.85 × 0.73 in",tts:"NeoSpeech Paul and Kate"},
["https://afb.org/aw/10/6/16088","https://www.nfbnet.org/pipermail/nfbcs_nfbnet.org/2011-November/003628.html","https://www.loc.gov/nls/services-and-resources/equipment-for-nls-materials/commercial-players-compatible-with-nls-materials-and-additional-products/"]);
D6("bksxt",["The BookSense XT is physically identical to the standard BookSense but adds an FM radio, 4 GB of built-in memory and Bluetooth headset support. GW Micro priced it at $499.","It is a candy-bar-shaped player, 4.25 × 1.85 × 0.73 inches and 4 ounces. Stereo speakers are at the top of the face, above a date-and-time button, a five-way control and a phone-style keypad with alternating raised and recessed keys.","The right side has Play/Pause (also power), Record and a lock switch. The left side has the Mode button, which on the XT also switches to the FM radio, plus volume keys and an SD slot. Microphone and headphone jacks are on top, and power and USB on the bottom.","AccessWorld tested an XT with software 1.1. It recommended the XT for anyone wanting a built-in FM radio, 4 GB of memory, Word document reading and the smallest size.","Users report MP3 or WAV recording, a 12-hour battery and voice-command control of playback."],
{price:"$499",wt:"4 oz",dim:"4.25 × 1.85 × 0.73 in"},
["https://afb.org/aw/10/6/16088","https://www.nfbnet.org/pipermail/nfbcs_nfbnet.org/2011-November/003628.html"]);
