const SOPR="https://press.aboutamazon.com/2019/9/amazon-introduces-an-array-of-new-devices-and-features-to-help-make-your-home-simpler-safer-and-smarter",SOV="https://www.theverge.com/2019/9/25/20883867/amazon-smart-oven-features-specs-price-release-date-alexa-echo-dot",SOMAN="https://customerdocumentation.s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Alexa+Devices/Amazon+Smart+Oven+Manual+(Accessible+HTML).html",SOHELP="https://www.amazon.com/b?ie=UTF8&node=21341314011",SOWOOT="https://www.utahsweetsavings.com/amazon-basics-eb544n-smart-oven-149-99-reg-249-99-today-only-2-08/",SORED="https://www.reddit.com/r/alexa/comments/1ngto3z/i_think_the_wifi_card_in_my_amazon_basics_smart/",SOREV="https://www.reviewed.com/cooking/features/amazon-smart-oven-is-it-worth-buying";
AD({id:"amzmicro",n:"AmazonBasics Microwave with Alexa (S9N29R)",co:"amazon",cat:M,yr:2018,y:"Announced September 20, 2018; shipped from November 14, 2018. Reported discontinued by 2025 (user reports on Reddit, September 2025).",
 o:"A $59.99 compact 700-watt microwave controlled by voice through any Echo device. It was Amazon's first kitchen appliance and the first device built on the Alexa Connect Kit.",
 h:"Amazon announced it at its September 20, 2018 devices event, alongside the second-generation Echo Show, Echo Wall Clock, Amazon Smart Plug and Echo Input. The Verge called it 'the e-commerce giant's first real kitchen appliance.' It was the first product built on the Alexa Connect Kit (ACK), a low-cost Wi-Fi and Bluetooth Low Energy module that lets ordinary appliances take commands from an existing Echo. The microwave itself has no microphone or speaker, and Amazon positioned it largely as a reference design for ACK. It also integrated Dash Replenishment, with an optional Auto Popcorn Replenishment feature that reorders popcorn. Tech press first treated a talking microwave as a gimmick. In January 2021 Forbes' Steven Aquino argued that the voice control made it genuinely accessible for people with visual or motor disabilities. AFB's AccessWorld reviewed it in December 2020, called it 'very accessible' when paired with an Alexa device and 'an excellent value,' and judged it a good choice of small accessible microwave. Users on NFB's Electronics-Talk list reported getting a braille keypad overlay for it in 2020. Its successor in the Alexa kitchen line was the 2019 Amazon Smart Oven (see that entry).",
 s:{price:"$59.99 at launch (2018); still $59.99 in AccessWorld's December 2020 review",pwr:"700 W output, 1,050 W input, 120 V / 60 Hz",cap:"0.7 cu ft, 10 in turntable",dim:"17.3 × 14.1 × 10.1 in",wt:"21.9 lb",conn:"Wi-Fi and Bluetooth Low Energy through the Alexa Connect Kit module (pairs as a smart-home device in the Alexa app)",disp:"Digital time readout; no speech of its own (spoken feedback comes from the Echo)",used:"No resale price found; Reddit users reported in 2025 that it can no longer be bought new"},
 f:["Alexa voice control through any Echo on the same Wi-Fi network, for example 'Alexa, microwave for 2 minutes' or 'Alexa, reheat coffee'","Raised 'Ask Alexa' button: press it and speak the command without saying the wake word","Voice power levels ('five minutes on power six' or 'on medium')","Quick-cook voice presets (potatoes, frozen vegetables, coffee and others)","Optional Dash Replenishment reordering of popcorn"],
 src:[VMW,AFTV,FONE,AFBMW,FORB,"http://www.nfbnet.org/pipermail/electronics-talk_nfbnet.org/2020-July/017897.html",SORED],
 desc:["The AmazonBasics Microwave is a small white countertop microwave. AccessWorld gives its outside dimensions as 17.3 inches wide, 14.1 inches deep and 10.1 inches high, and its weight as 21.9 pounds. Inside is a 0.7 cubic foot cavity with a 10-inch glass turntable. It is rated at 700 watts of cooking power, lower than most full-size microwaves, though owners on NFB's lists said food needs only a little longer.","The door is hinged on the left, and its handle runs down the strip just left of the control panel. All controls and the display sit in a strip about three inches wide on the right front. At the top is the digital time readout. Below it is a row of three buttons: Power Level, Time Defrost and Weight Defrost.","Next comes a standard keypad, numbers 1 to 9 in three rows. The row below has Popcorn on the left, 0 in the middle and Kitchen Timer on the right. The bottom row has Pause/Stop on the left, the Alexa button in the middle and Start/+30 Sec on the right. The Alexa button is the only raised button. The others are flat membrane keys marked only by small indentations, so AccessWorld suggests locator dots or braille labels for anyone who wants to use the keypad by touch.","To set up the turntable, you feel for three taped bumps on the cavity floor, remove the tape, and seat the roller ring around them. The glass tray's three bumps then lock loosely into place on the rollers. The microwave has no microphone or speaker. It pairs over Wi-Fi as a smart-home device in the Alexa app. AccessWorld's tester needed sighted help for one step of pairing, holding the 2 key for four seconds, because the keys are hard to identify by touch.","Once paired, it is controlled entirely by voice through any nearby Echo: 'Alexa, microwave for one minute,' 'add 30 seconds,' 'reheat coffee' or 'five minutes on medium.' Pressing the raised Alexa button and simply saying 'one minute' does the same thing without the wake word. Alexa answers through the Echo, which gives a blind cook the spoken confirmation the microwave itself cannot."]});
AD({id:"amzsmartoven",n:"Amazon Smart Oven / Amazon Basics 4-in-1 Smart Oven (EB544N)",co:"amazon",cat:M,yr:2019,y:"Announced September 25, 2019; shipped from November 14, 2019. Later sold under the Amazon Basics name (Woot listings as 'Amazon Basics EB544N Smart Oven'). No 2021 launch and no second-generation model were found; user reports from September 2025 describe it as discontinued, with Alexa connections failing.",
 o:"A 4-in-1 countertop appliance (microwave, convection oven, air fryer and food warmer) controlled by voice through an Echo. Amazon offers a free braille keypad overlay.",
 h:"Amazon announced it on September 25, 2019 as the follow-up to the 2018 AmazonBasics Microwave. It sold for $249.99 bundled with an Echo Dot and shipped on November 14, 2019. The launch press release called it 'Voice-Controlled 4-in-1 Oven' and paired it with the new 'Certified for Humans' program for easy-setup smart-home devices. Like the microwave, it has no microphone or speaker and was described by The Verge as a reference design for Alexa appliance APIs. New at launch were 30 cooking presets, a temperature probe, Alexa voice preheat and temperature setting, Alexa Announcements when preheating finishes or food is done, and 'scan-to-cook.' Scan-to-cook reads the barcode of hundreds of packaged foods (Whole Foods Market, 365, Gardein, Marie Callender's and others) with the Alexa app and loads the cooking program onto the oven. Amazon's help page states that a free braille keypad overlay is available and has instructions for applying it. The oven was later listed as the 'Amazon Basics EB544N Smart Oven,' including refurbished units on Woot at $149.99 to $189.99 against a $249.99 reference price. A 2021 launch date and a 2025 second generation were both searched for and not found. The model number stayed EB544N. In September 2025 several owners on Reddit reported that their 4-in-1 ovens and Basics microwaves had lost Alexa connectivity, and that the products could no longer be bought new.",
 s:{price:"$249.99 with an included Echo Dot (2019)",pwr:"Input 1,600 W; microwave output 1,000 W; convection output 1,400 W; keep-warm 1,300 W; 120 V / 60 Hz",cap:"1.5 cu ft, 13.6 in turntable; large enough for a 5 lb chicken",dim:"21.73 × 21.38 × 12.83 in",wt:"About 44.75 lb",conn:"Wi-Fi, set up through the Alexa app",used:"Refurbished units on Woot at $149.99 to $189.99 (reference price $249.99)"},
 f:["Microwave, convection bake, air fry and keep-warm modes, plus defrost, reheat, auto roast and preset cook","30+ built-in presets","Alexa voice control through any compatible Echo ('Alexa, bake for 25 minutes at 350 degrees')","Alexa Announcements when preheating finishes or food is done","Temperature probe that plugs into the inside wall; the display shows the probe temperature","Scan-to-cook for packaged foods with the Alexa app","Free braille keypad overlay from Amazon"],
 src:[SOPR,SOV,SOMAN,SOHELP,SOWOOT,SOREV,SORED,"https://www.indiatoday.in/technology/features/story/amazon-event-2019-echo-studio-echo-buds-all-new-echo-and-all-other-devices-announced-1603344-2019-09-26"],
 desc:["The Amazon Smart Oven is a large countertop box that looks like an oversized microwave. Its manual gives outside dimensions of 21.73 by 21.38 by 12.83 inches and a weight of about 44.75 pounds, roughly twice the size and weight of the 2018 AmazonBasics Microwave. The 1.5 cubic foot cavity has a 13.6-inch turntable and holds a 5-pound chicken.","It combines four appliances. As a microwave it puts out 1,000 watts, and as a convection oven 1,400 watts. Air frying is convection cooking on the tall rack with the air-fry pan, and keep-warm runs at 1,300 watts. The whole unit draws up to 1,600 watts from a standard 120-volt outlet.","Accessories include a tall rack, a short rack, a perforated air-fry tray and a temperature probe. The manual explains which go with which mode: the tall rack and air-fry tray for air fry and convect, the short rack for keep warm, and the probe required for auto roast. No racks or metal may be used in the microwave, defrost, reheat or preset-cook modes. The probe plugs into a socket on the inside wall, and the display then shows its reading.","The front has a control panel with a display, a keypad and mode keys such as Convect. Amazon offers a free braille keypad overlay that marks the key functions, with instructions on its help page for applying it. As with the microwave, the oven has no microphone or speaker, so all speech comes from a nearby Echo.","By voice you can preheat, set the temperature, start, stop and add time, for example 'Alexa, bake for 25 minutes at 350 degrees.' Alexa announces when the oven has preheated and when food is done, so a blind cook does not need to read the display. With scan-to-cook, the Alexa app reads a packaged food's barcode and loads the right program. Owners reported in 2025 that Alexa control had stopped working on some units and that Amazon no longer sells the oven new."]});
// Batch 15: talking scale, thermometers and clock (Miscellaneous)
const AWK="https://afb.org/aw/21/11/17296",AWG="https://afb.org/aw/18/11/15267",AWT="https://afb.org/aw/13/5/15813",MXV="https://www.maxiaids.com/product/vox-2-talking-kitchen-scale2",MXR="https://www.maxiaids.com/product/reizen-talking-atomic-alarm-clock",BBT="https://theblindbridge.com/shopping/talking-thermometer/";
AC({id:"myweigh",n:"My Weigh",f:"Founding year not found on the Maxi-Aids listing used",h:"Maker of digital scales, including the Vox talking kitchen scales that AccessWorld has recommended in its gift guides since 2018.",src:[MXV,AWG]});
AC({id:"thermoworks",n:"ThermoWorks",f:"Founding year not given in the sources used",h:"A US maker of cooking and food-safety thermometers. Its RT8400 talking thermometer was reviewed by AccessWorld in 2012, and blind cooks still recommend it in 2026 (The Blind Bridge).",src:[AWT,BBT]});
AC({id:"ultmost",n:"Ultmost",f:"Founding year not found",h:"Maker of the HE-113-EN talking digital body thermometer, sold as the TALKiNG Fast Reading Thermometer. AccessWorld noted that the packaging did not name the maker; it identified Ultmost through a search.",src:[AWT]});
AC({id:"grahamfield",n:"Graham-Field (Lumiscope)",f:"Founding year not given in the sources used",h:"A medical-supply company whose Lumiscope brand made the Talking Digital Ear Thermometer reviewed by AccessWorld in 2012.",src:[AWT]});
AC({id:"reizen",n:"Reizen",f:"Founding year not found",h:"A brand of low-vision daily-living aids sold through Maxi-Aids, LS&S and others, including talking clocks, watches and magnifiers.",src:[MXR,"https://lssproducts.com/clocks-watches/talking-clocks/"]});
AD({id:"vox2",n:"Vox-2 Talking Kitchen Scale",co:"myweigh",cat:M,yr:0,y:"Current (Maxi-Aids listing, 2025 reviews). AccessWorld's 2018 and 2021 guides name a 'Vox 20' ($42 and $42.99); its relationship to the Vox-2 is not stated.",
 o:"A talking kitchen scale that announces weights in four languages, up to 11 lb (5 kg) in 1 g steps.",
 h:"AccessWorld's 2018 holiday guide listed the Vox 20 at $42, describing it as speaking five languages and weighing up to 11 pounds. Its 2021 accessible-kitchen guide listed it at $42.99 with four languages (English, German, French, Spanish) and tare, and noted that BlindMiceMart's Dale uses it to calculate postage. The current Maxi-Aids listing is for the Vox-2, made by My Weigh.",
 s:{price:"$46.95 at Maxi-Aids (list $59.95); the Vox 20 was $42 to $42.99 in 2018 to 2021",bat:"4 × AA (included); auto-off after 90 seconds",dim:"9.0 × 6.5 × 1.75 in",wt:"1.0 lb",cap:"11 lb (5 kg) in 0.1 oz (1 g) steps",disp:"Large blue-backlit LCD"},
 f:["Speaks weights in English, German, French or Spanish","Grams or pounds and ounces","Hi/Low volume, and speech can be turned off","Tare to subtract a bowl or container","Oversized 1.5 × 1.2 in keys"],
 src:[MXV,AWK,AWG],
 desc:["The Vox-2 is a flat kitchen scale made for blind and low-vision cooks. Maxi-Aids lists it at 9.0 by 6.5 by 1.75 inches and 1 pound. It has a gray case and a stainless-steel weighing platform that wipes clean.","The controls are oversized keys, each about 1.5 by 1.2 inches, black with white print. One owner said they are big and easy to identify by touch. A large LCD with blue backlighting shows the weight for anyone with some vision.","Place an item on the platform and the scale speaks its weight. It weighs up to 11 pounds (5 kg) in 1 gram (0.1 ounce) steps, in grams or in pounds and ounces. Tare zeroes out a bowl so only the ingredient is weighed.","It speaks English, German, French or Spanish, with a high/low volume switch, and speech can be switched off. One 2025 reviewer described the voice as clear and polite, saying 'Hello' and 'Goodbye' at power on and off. The same reviewer noted that it does not announce the unit mode, and that it beeps rapidly before speaking a negative weight when a container is lifted off.","It runs on four AA batteries (included) and turns itself off after 90 seconds idle. The reviewer also wished the print manual came in large print or electronic form. Maxi-Aids lists a lifetime limited warranty (30 years in Europe)."]});
AD({id:"rt8400",n:"ThermoWorks RT8400 Talking Thermometer",co:"thermoworks",cat:M,yr:0,y:"On the market by 2012 (AccessWorld review); still recommended in 2026",o:"A talking probe thermometer for food, liquids and bath water, from −58 to 572 °F.",
 h:"AccessWorld's Janet Ingber reviewed it in 2012 at $36.00 from Blind Mice Mart. The Blind Bridge (October 2026) calls it the talking thermometer blind cooks write in about, priced up to about $60.",
 s:{price:"$36.00 (AccessWorld, 2012); about $60 in 2026 (The Blind Bridge)",bat:"2 × AAA (included); auto-off after 10 minutes",dim:"About 9 in long with a 5 in metal probe",disp:"Display window plus spoken readout",cap:"−58 to 572 °F, accurate to within 1.8 °F per the manual"},
 src:[AWT,BBT],
 desc:["The RT8400 is a long, thin probe thermometer about 9 inches long. The upper part is plastic and the lower 5 inches is a slender, pointed metal probe. A clip-on cover protects the probe and lets you hang the thermometer up.","At the top is a loop for hanging it on a hook. Below that are a small display window, a single large oval button and the speaker openings. The oval button is the only control you need, and it is easy to find by touch.","On the back, the battery compartment at the top opens with a small loop and holds two AAA batteries (included). Below it a small switch selects Fahrenheit or Celsius; it ships set to Fahrenheit.","Press the oval button and it beeps within a couple of seconds. Insert the probe and press again, and it speaks the temperature almost at once. It shuts itself off after ten idle minutes. AccessWorld measured boiling water at 211.5 °F and ice water at 33.4 °F, and found the first reading of a session slightly less accurate than later ones.","Its range is −58 to 572 °F, accurate to within 1.8 °F per the manual. Uses include checking that chicken is cooked through or that a child's bath water is safe. AccessWorld's one complaint was that the manual was not accessible out of the box."]});
AD({id:"talkingthermo",n:"TALKiNG Fast Reading Thermometer (Ultmost HE-113-EN)",co:"ultmost",cat:M,yr:0,y:"On the market by 2012 (AccessWorld review)",o:"An inexpensive talking body thermometer for oral, underarm or rectal use.",
 s:{price:"$12.95 (AccessWorld, 2012); talking body thermometers about $13 in 2018 (AccessWorld)",bat:"2 × AG13 button cells (pre-installed); auto-off after 3 minutes",disp:"Small display window plus female voice"},
 src:[AWT,AWG],
 desc:["This is a slim stick-shaped digital thermometer that speaks body temperature. AccessWorld found that its packaging did not name the maker; a search identified it as the Ultmost HE-113-EN.","Hold it with the probe tip pointing down. The broad top section has a display window and two buttons. The upper button repeats the last temperature, and the lower one is power. A small speaker is inside.","The mouth end has a removable plastic protective cover and a flexible tip. The battery compartment on the back is closed by two tiny screws and holds two AG13 button cells, already installed.","Press power and a clear female voice says 'Ready.' It beeps every few seconds while measuring, then announces 'Your body temperature is' and the reading. Holding the power button switches it from Fahrenheit to Celsius, and it turns itself off after three minutes.","AccessWorld found it accurate against non-talking thermometers read by a sighted helper, and more consistent than the Lumiscope ear thermometer. It criticized the lack of an accessible manual or usable website. It cost $12.95."]});
AD({id:"lumiscopeear",n:"Lumiscope Talking Digital Ear Thermometer",co:"grahamfield",cat:M,yr:0,y:"On the market by 2012 (AccessWorld review)",o:"A fast talking ear thermometer that also speaks the time.",
 s:{price:"$32.85 (AccessWorld, 2012); spare probe covers $7.99 per 40",bat:"2 × AAA (included)",disp:"Display window covering most of the front, plus speech"},
 src:[AWT],
 desc:["The Lumiscope is an oval handheld ear thermometer. A large ridged button on top turns it on and takes a reading once the probe is in the ear. The probe is on the back. It comes with a stand and ten disposable probe covers.","A display window covers most of the front. Below it are two buttons, Memo and Talk, for stepping through earlier readings. Pressing Talk while it is on speaks the time, because when idle it works as a clock. Setting that clock needs sighted help.","Small recessed holes on the sides are pressed with an unbent paper clip: one labeled C/F on the right switches Fahrenheit and Celsius, and one on the left resets the unit. Two AAA batteries go in a compartment held by a tiny screw just below the probe.","To take a reading, you slide a clean cover over the probe, insert it gently into the ear canal and press the top button. It beeps, plays a short melody and speaks the temperature. The manual asks for 30 minutes in the room first, and ten minutes between readings in the same ear.","AccessWorld found it very fast, but it read consistently higher than the TALKiNG oral thermometer, by as much as three degrees during a fever. The reviewer advised checking with a doctor when accuracy matters."]});
AD({id:"reizenatomic",n:"Reizen Talking Atomic Alarm Clock",co:"reizen",cat:M,yr:0,y:"Current (Maxi-Aids listing, 2026)",o:"A radio-controlled talking alarm clock that sets itself from the US atomic time signal.",
 h:"Maxi-Aids lists it at $44.95 (list $55.00). LS&S sells a comparable Talking Radio Controlled Atomic Clock (SKU 101021) at $42.95.",
 s:{price:"$44.95 at Maxi-Aids (list $55.00)",bat:"2 × AA (not included); low-battery reminder",disp:"LCD, 1¾ × 3 in, showing time with seconds, month, date, day of week and alarm time",conn:"Receives the US atomic time radio signal broadcast from Colorado; 8 time zones"},
 f:["Female voice announces time and date","Optional hourly announcements from 8 AM to 10 PM","Automatic daylight-saving changes","5-minute alarm with beeps and spoken time; 5-minute snooze","High/low volume switch","Voice guidance during setup; press a button 3 times to hear the time zone"],
 src:[MXR,"https://lssproducts.com/clocks-watches/talking-clocks/"],
 desc:["The Reizen Talking Atomic Alarm Clock is a battery-powered talking clock that never needs setting. It picks up the radio time signal broadcast from the US atomic clock in Colorado, and changes for daylight saving time on its own.","Press its talk button and a natural-sounding female voice announces the time and date. It can also announce every hour from 8 AM to 10 PM, which can be turned off. A high/low volume switch helps people with hearing loss.","The front has an LCD about 1¾ by 3 inches showing the time with running seconds, the month, date, day of the week and the alarm time. The retailer listings used do not give the case's dimensions or button layout.","The alarm sounds for an extra-long five minutes, beeping and announcing the current time, and a snooze gives five more minutes. Newer units add eight selectable time zones (Pacific, Mountain, Central, Eastern, Alaska, Hawaii, Atlantic and UTC). Pressing a button three times speaks the current zone.","It runs on two AA batteries, which are not included. When batteries go in, it plays a self-introduction, and the voice then guides setup. It also warns when the batteries are low."]});
// Batch 16: Braille EDGE 40 and Smart Beetle descriptions
const AWEDGE="https://afb.org/aw/18/4/15220",AWSB="https://afb.org/aw/19/2/15123",AVSB="https://www.applevis.com/reviews/smart-beetle-hims";
U(DEV,"edge",{s:{price:"$2,795 (AccessWorld, April 2017)",dim:"12.2 × 4 × 0.86 in",wt:"1.73 lb"},src:[AWEDGE],
 ha:"AccessWorld (April 2017, republished from Blind Bargains) compared it with BAUM's VarioUltra 40 ($3,995) as a 'smart display': more than a terminal, with notepad, scheduler and other internal applications, but less than a full notetaker. At that time HIMS offered no smaller EDGE.",
 desc:["The Braille EDGE 40 is a 40-cell refreshable braille display from HIMS, which AccessWorld classed as a 'smart display' with its own small applications. It measures 12.2 inches long, 4 inches deep and 0.86 inches thick, and weighs 1.73 pounds, a little wider and heavier than BAUM's VarioUltra 40.","Along the edge nearest you runs the 40-cell braille line with its cursor-routing buttons. Behind it is an eight-key Perkins-style keyboard, with keys for dots 1 to 8 in their usual positions. The keys sit close together, so people with large hands may find them crowded.","The space bar sits between dots 1 and 4. On each side of it are rectangular function keys, F1 to F4 among them, whose jobs change with context. In the internal programs F4 opens the menus, and with JAWS F2 acts as the Alt key. Sets of arrow keys sit on either side of the Perkins keyboard for navigation.","Around the edges are an SD card slot, a jack for the included AC adapter and a mini-USB port. The USB port gives slow charging and connects the EDGE to a PC or Mac as a screen-reader terminal. AccessWorld found it cannot read files from a thumb drive, and its SD card cannot be read from another device through the EDGE.","It comes in a zip-up carrying bag with a strap, shaped like a small laptop bag, which must be closed to secure the unit, so the display can't be used while inside. HIMS sold an Executive Products case for $98 that lets you use the display fully while it stays protected. In 2017 it sold for $2,795."]});
U(DEV,"sbeetle",{s:{price:"$1,345 list; on sale for $995 in January 2018 (AccessWorld)",bat:"About 18 hours (AccessWorld)",conn:"5 Bluetooth plus 1 USB (micro-USB) connections; Bluetooth keyboard emulation"},src:[AWSB,AVSB],
 ha:"Scott Davert's AppleVis review (December 2015) gave it 4 stars. He noted that it was natively supported in iOS 9 and OS X El Capitan, while earlier iOS versions treated it as an 18-cell BrailleSense, so panning skipped text. AccessWorld (2018) found that it had not been updated in a long time, and that the manual's keyboard-emulation section was incomplete.",
 desc:["The Smart Beetle is a 14-cell braille display from HIMS with no notetaking applications, only a menu and a terminal clipboard. AppleVis's reviewer found it about the size of an iPhone 6S but a little thicker and wider, almost exactly the size of a Focus 14. It is a rectangle with rounded edges.","With the braille line toward you, the 14 cells run along the front of the top surface, with a diamond-shaped panning button at each end. Directly behind the cells are 14 cursor-routing buttons.","Behind those, on a slightly raised level, is the space bar with two rectangular keys on each side: F1 and F2 on the left, F3 and F4 on the right. Behind them is the eight-key Perkins-style keyboard. At the very back of the top are a small speaker for audio alerts and a couple of indicator lights.","The back panel has a reset button at the far left and a micro-USB port on the right. The left side and front are bare. The power button is alone on the right side: a quick press opens the menu, which offers Bluetooth serial, USB or Bluetooth keyboard modes, and a three-second press turns it on or off. Pairing with iOS needs no PIN.","It connects to up to five Bluetooth devices plus one USB device at once, and battery life is about 18 hours. It comes with a thin, close-fitting case that closes magnetically, a micro-USB cable, print and braille guides, and a driver CD. It listed at $1,345 and was on sale for $995 in early 2018."]});
// Batch 17: Plextalk Pocket PTP1, PAC Mate BX400/QX400, VoiceSense descriptions
const AWP10="https://afb.org/aw/10/6/16088",AWP13="https://afb.org/aw/13/5/15814",PXJP="https://www.plextalk.com/jp/support/ptp1/specifications/",AVH="https://www.avh.asso.fr/implantations/certam/evaluations-de-produits/lecteur-enregistreur-plextalk-pocket-ptp1";
U(DEV,"ptp1",{s:{price:"$349 (AccessWorld, 2010); $275 promotional price until May 31, 2012; €350 in France (2013); ¥41,040 tax-exempt in Japan",dim:"112 × 55 × 16 mm (4.4 × 2.2 × 0.6 in)",wt:"About 110 g (3.9 oz) with battery; carrying case 51 g",bat:"Removable lithium-ion, 1,430 mAh; about 10 h DAISY/MP3 playback, about 8 h recording; about 4 h to charge",sto:"SD/SDHC up to 32 GB (2 GB card included); USB OTG for memory sticks and external CD drives"},src:[AWP10,AWP13,PXJP,AVH],
 ha:"AccessWorld first evaluated it in 2010 at $349; its TTS used the Nuance Tom and Samantha voices. Deborah Kendrick's 2012 evaluation praised its stability and DAISY recording. Shinano Kenshi's Japanese specification page notes that the white model ended production in November 2013, after which it was sold in black only.",
 desc:["The PLEXTALK Pocket PTP1 is a DAISY book player and recorder from Shinano Kenshi. AccessWorld described it as shaped like a deck of cards with a quarter of the cards missing. It measures 112 by 55 by 16 millimeters (4.4 by 2.2 by 0.6 inches) and weighs about 110 grams with its battery. It was made in white and black; white ended in 2013.","The face is exceptionally smooth. On the lower part is a standard 12-key phone-style keypad, with 11 more keys above it. Every key has its own shape and texture, so AccessWorld found them effortless to locate by touch. Most prominent is a ring of four arrow keys around a concave Enter button, with Go To, Bookmark, Menu and Title keys at its four corners. Above the keys is a speaker that AccessWorld's reviewer called the best she had heard on a handheld player.","On the top edge are jacks for headphones and an external microphone. The bottom edge has the USB port and the AC adapter jack. The SD card slot is on the left edge. On the right edge are an up-and-down volume switch and a key-lock switch. A built-in microphone lets you record without accessories.","It plays DAISY books from NLS BARD, Bookshare, Learning Ally and Audible, as well as MP3, WAV and AMR-WB+ music, and reads text, HTML and Word files with TTS. It records in DAISY format with headings you can insert during or after recording. Holding Record captures a one-minute voice memo. A removable 1,430 mAh lithium-ion battery gives about 10 hours of playback and charges in about 4 hours. Melodic sounds confirm commands, and pressing 0 opens the full user manual.","In the box were a 2 GB SD card, stereo earbuds, an AC adapter, a USB cable, a short USB adapter for memory sticks or a CD drive, the manual on CD, and a zippered padded pouch. It cost $349 in 2010."]});
const BXM="https://www2.freedomscientific.com/fs_support/PAC_Mate_BX/Docs/PAC_Mate_Owner's_Manual_BX.txt",QXM="https://www2.freedomscientific.com/fs_support/PAC_Mate_QX/Docs/PAC_Mate_Owner's_Manual_QX.txt",BXI="https://www2.freedomscientific.com/fs_products/PACmate_bx400info.asp",QXI="https://www2.freedomscientific.com/fs_products/PACmate_qx400info.asp",AWPM="https://www.afb.org/aw/6/3/14628";
U(DEV,"pacmate",{s:{cpu:"Intel XScale 400 MHz",ram:"64 MB",sto:"32 MB flash ROM; 2 CompactFlash Type II slots",os:"Windows CE .NET 4.2 with Pocket PC 2003; JAWS for Pocket PC; Eloquence speech",dim:"BX400: 11 × 4.87 × 1.93 in (BX420 with 20-cell display: 11 × 6.81 × 1.93 in); QX400: 12.3 × 6.27 × 1.62 in (QX420: 12.3 × 8.21 × 1.62 in)",wt:"BX400: 1 lb 13 oz (0.82 kg); BX420: 3 lb 9 oz; QX400: 2 lb (0.90 kg); QX420: 3 lb 12 oz; about 4 lb with a 40-cell display (AccessWorld)",bat:"Lithium-polymer, about 4 h to recharge; warnings at 20% and 12%"},src:[BXM,QXM,BXI,QXI,AWPM],
 ha:"Both owner's manuals are dated December 2003. AccessWorld (2005) noted that the PAC Mate was originally released in 2002 and was then in its second generation. Unlike its competitors, its braille display could be detached and used with JAWS or Window-Eyes on a desktop. AccessWorld praised that feature and the access to third-party Pocket PC software, but criticized inconsistent contracted braille and occasional instability.",
 desc:["The PAC Mate BX400 and QX400 are Pocket PC-based notetakers from Freedom Scientific, running JAWS on Windows CE. The BX400 has a braille keyboard and measures 11 by 4.87 by 1.93 inches and 1 pound 13 ounces. The QX400 has a QWERTY keyboard and measures 12.3 by 6.27 by 1.62 inches and 2 pounds. All keys are on top, and all ports and switches are on the back.","The BX400 keyboard has eight braille keys, ordered left to right 7, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6 and 8, with a space bar near the front center. Between keys 1 and 4 sits a four-way 'cursor cross' for the arrows. Above each braille key is a matching function key: F7, F3, F2, F1, F4, F5, F6 and F8. F1 is Escape, F2 the menu bar, F4 the Start menu and F6 FSEdit. The speaker is just left of F7 and the internal microphone just right of F8.","On the back panel, from right to left, are the On/Off switch, the power jack, a mini A/B USB port, two CompactFlash Type II slots, an infrared port, a 2.5 mm microphone jack and a 3.5 mm headset jack. CompactFlash cards go in one way only and click gently into place.","On the bottom, left of the left middle rubber foot, is a small round reset button; pressing it erases all data. Across from it on the right is the release for detaching the braille display or drawer. A 20- or 40-cell PAC Mate Portable Braille Display slides onto the front and locks in. With a 40-cell display the BX weighs about 4 pounds, and without it just under 2 pounds.","Inside is a 400 MHz Intel XScale processor with 64 MB of RAM and 32 MB of flash. A lithium-polymer battery recharges in about 4 hours and announces warnings at 20 and 12 percent. It runs Pocket PC 2003 with Freedom Scientific's FSEdit, FSCalc and Stopwatch, with JAWS and Eloquence speech."]});
U(DEV,"vsense",{s:{cpu:"Intel PXA270, 540 MHz",ram:"128 MB RAM plus 64 MB OS storage",sto:"1 GB user flash; SD and CF slots",os:"Windows CE .NET 5.0",dim:"18.8 × 7.7 × 2.5 cm (7.4 × 3.0 × 0.98 in)",wt:"266 g (0.58 lb)",bat:"Detachable, user-replaceable lithium-polymer, 12 hours",price:"About $1,800 (elektronika.lt, 2007); about $2,150 (PCNews.ru)"},src:["https://www.freelists.org/post/gps-talkusers/Voicenote-or-Voice-Sense,31","https://www.elektronika.lt/naujienos/kompiuterija/8635/voicesense-kompiuteris-akliesiems/","https://pcnews.ru/news/gps-gw-micro-voicesense-windows-ce-net-540-pxa270-fm-wi-fi-802-11-bluetooth-usb-sd-cf-12-188-77-25-181367.html","https://afb.org/aw/12/1/15994"],
 ha:"GW Micro distributed it in the US. A user quoting GW Micro's spec page in October 2007 listed the hardware, and Sendero GPS was due for it within weeks. After HIMS and GW Micro split in 2011, HIMS sold it directly from Austin, Texas, alongside a QWERTY VoiceSense.",
 desc:["The VoiceSense is a speech-only notetaker from HIMS with a braille keyboard and no braille display. GW Micro's published specifications gave its size as 18.8 by 7.7 by 2.5 centimeters (about 7.4 by 3 by 1 inch) and its weight as 266 grams, just over half a pound.","The top carries a Perkins-style braille keyboard. The sources found do not describe the remaining controls in detail.","For sound it has stereo speakers and a headphone jack. For input it has a built-in condenser microphone and a microphone jack, used for recording, and there is an FM radio receiver. The exact placement of the jacks is not given in the sources found.","Its ports are USB On-The-Go (USB 2.0 compatible), an SD card slot and a CompactFlash slot. Wireless is 802.11b/g Wi-Fi and Bluetooth 1.1. Inside is a 540 MHz Intel PXA270 processor with 128 MB of RAM, 64 MB of OS storage and 1 GB of user flash, running Windows CE .NET 5.0.","A detachable, user-replaceable lithium-polymer battery gives about 12 hours of continuous use. 2007 news reports put the price at about $1,800 to $2,150. The later VoiceSense QWERTY used the smooth white plastic shell of other HIMS products, and at CSUN 2010 GW Micro's Dan Weirich dropped one on the floor and kept using it."]});
// Batch 18: Hansone, SyncBraille, QBraille XL, Braille eMotion, LevelStar Icon, PAC Mate Omni
U(DEV,"hansone",{s:{os:"Windows CE .NET",ram:"64 MB RAM",sto:"64 MB flash; two CompactFlash slots",disp:"32 cells with 32 cursor-routing buttons",dim:"9 × 5 × 1.5 in",wt:"2.7 lb",bat:"Detachable lithium-ion",io:"Infrared, 2 USB, LAN, parallel, video out, serial, mic and stereo headphone jacks"},src:["https://afb.org/aw/6/1/14662","https://afb.org/aw/6/1/14661","https://afb.org/aw/6/1/14658","https://afb.org/aw/6/3/14624"],
 ha:"AccessWorld (January 2005) explained that 'Hansone' means 'to see the world through one fingertip' in Korean. Because the meaning did not carry over to English speakers, HIMS renamed it Braille SENSE, and GW Micro distributed it in the United States. AFB rated it 2.5 of 5 for documentation, 3.5 for word processing, 3.0 for the web browser, calendar and address list, and 4.0 for speech and braille quality. Reviewers reported a confusing, poorly written manual, occasional freezes and unreliable web connections. AFB noted that the rating applied to the unit tested and that the product was updated afterwards.",
 desc:["The Braille Hansone is a HIMS braille notetaker with both speech and refreshable braille, later sold as the Braille Sense. AccessWorld measured it at 9 by 5 by 1.5 inches and 2.7 pounds. It runs Windows CE .NET with 64 MB of RAM and 64 MB of flash.","Across the top is an 8-key braille keyboard; dot 7 is Backspace and dot 8 is Enter. There are four oval function keys: F1 and F2 to the left of the space bar, and F3 and F4 to the right. F1 opens the Program menu, F2 the current program's menu, F3 is Tab and F4 is Escape. A small LCD sits in the middle above the keyboard. Above the Enter and Backspace keys, at the two outer edges, are stereo speakers.","Nearest you is a 32-cell braille display, with a row of 32 cursor-routing buttons directly above it. At the right end of that row is an Advance key for scrolling forward, and at the left end a Scroll Back key.","On the front panel, from left to right, are the microphone jack, the stereo headphone jack, and five recorder buttons: Previous, Record, Stop, Play/Pause and Next. On the right side, from front to back, are the on/off switch, the AC adapter jack and the compartment for the detachable lithium-ion battery. The left side has two CompactFlash slots.","The back panel, from left to right, holds an infrared port, two USB ports, a LAN network jack, a parallel port, a video output and a serial port. There is no modem. It can also serve as a braille display or speech synthesizer for a computer, and it plays MP3 files."]});
const SYNCM="https://www.boundlessat.com/site/images/SyncBraille_User_Manual.pdf";
U(DEV,"syncb",{s:{disp:"20 cells (B20N) or 32 cells (B32N)",dim:"32-cell: 26 × 8.5 × 2 cm; 20-cell: 18.3 × 8.5 × 2 cm",wt:"32-cell: 350 g; 20-cell: 280 g",io:"USB 2.0 (USB-powered)",warranty:"1 year"},src:[SYNCM],
 ha:"SyncBraille is the HIMS brand name for its small USB braille displays, models B20N (20 cells) and B32N (32 cells). The manual pairs it with the speech-only Voice Sense notetaker, which announces 'SyncBraille 32 is connected' in speech and braille, and with Windows screen readers through a driver on the included CD.",
 desc:["SyncBraille is a slim USB braille display from HIMS made in 20-cell and 32-cell sizes. The 32-cell model measures 26 by 8.5 by 2 centimeters and weighs 350 grams. The 20-cell model is 18.3 by 8.5 by 2 centimeters and 280 grams. Both are only 2 centimeters thick.","The top surface carries the row of braille cells. Just behind the cells is a row of cursor keys, used to move the cursor to that position when editing.","At each end of the display are two round scroll keys, one upper and one lower. With a PC screen reader, the right pair are the Right Up and Right Down scroll keys and the left pair the Left Up and Left Down arrow keys. Pressed together they give Home, End, Enter, Tab, Shift+Tab, Alt and Alt+F4. With a Voice Sense, the upper keys scroll back and the lower keys scroll forward.","A small USB port on the right side is the only connector, and it has no dust cap, so the manual warns against dust and moisture. The manual describes no battery or power switch; the unit connects to a PC or a Voice Sense by USB cable.","The package contains the display, a user manual, a USB cable, a driver CD and a carrying case. HIMS gave it a one-year warranty."]});
const QXL="https://afb.org/aw/21/9/17172",Q40="https://afb.org/aw/22/1/17349";
U(DEV,"qbxl",{s:{price:"$3,195 (AccessWorld 2020)",bat:"16 to 18 hours (AccessWorld)",sto:"SD/SDHC up to 32 GB (2 GB card included)",io:"USB-C; up to 6 Bluetooth connections plus 1 USB",files:"Reads BRF, BRL, TXT, RTF, DOCX, PDF, DAISY text; saves TXT and BRL"},src:[QXL,Q40],
 ha:"Scott Davert reviewed it for AccessWorld in September 2020 on firmware 2.4. He found the braille sharp but a little spongy, and battery life of 16 to 18 hours. He had trouble with JAWS over Bluetooth and could not switch past more than one window with Alt+Tab. In AccessWorld's January 2021 comparison of 40-cell displays he reported that HIMS's November 2020 update had fixed the JAWS Bluetooth issue. He noted that it was the only one of the four with one-handed mode and that a support request went unanswered.",
 desc:["The QBraille XL is a 40-cell braille display from HIMS with a hybrid keyboard. It combines a Perkins-style braille keyboard with the function, modifier, navigation and arrow keys of a laptop, so screen reader commands can be typed as on a PC. It cost $3,195 in 2020.","With the braille display toward you, the 40 cells and their cursor-routing buttons run along the near edge, with two scroll buttons at each end. On the right side, the USB-C port is nearest you and an SD card slot is behind it. A recessed Hard Reset button on the back edge needs a paperclip. The Power/Sleep/Wake button is on the left side; a short press sleeps or wakes it, and holding it for five seconds turns it on or off.","The keys are not in neat rows. At the far left, nearest you, is the Mode key that toggles Hybrid Mode, with Pairing above it and Escape above that. To their right is a column of Shift, Caps Lock, Tab and F1, then F2 through F12 across the top. Every fourth function key has a tactile mark, with a gap between groups of four. Right of F12 is a six-key block: Insert, Home and End on the left, and Delete, Page Up and Page Down on the right. Beneath it are the arrow keys.","The bottom key row reads Control, Function, Windows, Alt, Space, Alt, Applications, Control in Microsoft layout, or Function, Control, Option, Command, Space, Command, Option, Control in Apple layout. Above that row are the eight Perkins braille keys. AppleVis users noted that the space bar sits high, above the braille cells, and that the cells have tactile ridges around them.","The box includes a 2 GB SD card, a USB-C cable, a power adapter, the first two chapters of the manual in braille, and a sturdy carrying case with shoulder strap and magnetic closure. Built-in apps include a notepad, DAISY reader, clock, stopwatch, alarm, calendar and calculator. It has no Wi-Fi."]});
U(DEV,"emotion",{src:["https://nfb.org/images/nfb/publications/bm/bm25/bm2506/bm250606.htm","https://striveability.uk/2025/03/30/whats-causing-a-commotion/"],
 ha:"The Braille Monitor (NFB) compared it with the HumanWare Brailliant BI 40X in 2025. Both start in about 20 seconds, both connect to five Bluetooth devices and one USB device, and both download books from Online DAISY, Bookshare, NFB-NEWSLINE and NLS braille. The eMotion adds a media player, a downloadable BARD Mobile app and legacy braille drivers for Android versions before 15. Selvas makes only a 40-cell version.",
 desc:["The Braille eMotion is a 40-cell smart braille display from Selvas BLV, formerly HIMS. It has a Perkins-style keyboard and built-in apps: a text editor, file manager, calculator, book downloads, a media player and a recorder. It is the only size Selvas makes.","The front edge has two panning keys, one at each end, with F1 through F4 between them from left to right. In the middle is a Home button with a microphone on each side. Every front control except Home and the microphones is angled slightly toward the top surface.","The right side has the volume buttons and a Mode button that changes their function, with the headphone jack and USB-C port behind them. On the left edge is a USB-A port near the front, with a Record button behind it.","On top, the braille display runs along the front edge, with the Spacebar, Control and Alt keys in front of it. Each cell has a cursor-routing key, and capsule-shaped panning buttons sit at both ends. It was the first Selvas display that pans with either those end buttons or the front keys. Behind the display is the Perkins keyboard, with a center key between dots 1 and 4.","Speakers sit at the two back corners, with the Power button just left of the right speaker. Just right of the left speaker are three small keys labeled W, B and C in braille. They toggle Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, or hold them to open settings, and the C key opens the Connectivity app. It also has a microSD slot."]});
const ICON="https://afb.org/aw/8/4/14333";
U(DEV,"icon",{s:{dim:"5.09 × 3.35 × 0.975 in",wt:"7.4 oz",bat:"Rechargeable lithium-ion"},src:[ICON],
 ha:"Jay Leventhal reviewed it for AccessWorld in 2007 at $1,395. He praised the intuitive menus and Bookshare integration, which stored your password and unpacked books on download. He criticized slow text entry, limited contact and planner fields, poor table reading and its start-up beeps.",
 desc:["The LevelStar Icon is a Linux-based PDA for blind users. It measures 5.09 by 3.35 by 0.975 inches and 7.4 ounces. It has a 30-gigabyte hard drive and Wi-Fi. It plays MP3 and DAISY books, records voice, and includes a web browser, e-mail, an address book, a planner, a word processor, a calculator and a stopwatch.","On the top edge, from left to right, are an external microphone jack, a miniSD slot, a headphone jack and a headset jack. Stereo speakers sit at the top of the left and right sides. Further down the left side are the volume up and down control, a button that switches between the speakers and the mono earpiece, and the Voice Record button.","At the bottom of both the left and right sides are buttons that open the battery compartment for the lithium-ion battery. On the bottom edge is the Power/Sync cable port.","On the front, at the bottom, is a standard 12-key telephone keypad. Directly above the 1, 2 and 3 keys are OK, Menu and Cancel; holding Cancel turns the Icon on. Above that row is a large diamond-shaped Arrow key; you press its top, bottom, left or right edge to move. A small Select key is in its middle, with the Status key to its left and the Help key to its right.","The top row of keys, from left to right, holds the hot key, the Applications Menu key and the Wi-Fi On/Off key. The package included an AC adapter, a USB cable, a case, a CD with the manual and an HTML Quick Start guide, and a braille Quick Start guide."]});
const OMQX="https://support.freedomscientific.com/content/Documents/Manuals/PAC-Mate/PAC-Mate-Omni-QX-Owners-Manual.pdf",OMF="https://www.freedomscientific.com/Content/Documents/ProductFlyers/PAC_Mate_Omni_Flyer.pdf";
U(DEV,"pacomni",{s:{cpu:"Intel XScale 400 MHz",ram:"64 MB",sto:"128 MB flash ROM; 2 CompactFlash slots",dim:"QX400: 12.3 × 6.27 × 1.62 in (31.24 × 15.93 × 4.11 cm)",wt:"QX400: 2 lb (0.90 kg)",power:"12 V AC adapter/charger"},src:[OMQX,OMF],
 ha:"Freedom Scientific's launch flyer presented the Omni as the newest generation of the PAC Mate. It ran Windows Mobile 6.0, which Freedom Scientific called the most keyboard-friendly version yet, with full soft-key support. The flyer says the new hardware 'does not appreciably change its outer appearance.' Because all data is stored in flash, files survive a reset or a flat battery. It came in six models: BX400, BX420, BX440, QX400, QX420 and QX440. The flyer also lists Mobile Office including PowerPoint, Direct Push e-mail, Windows Live Search and Messenger voice clips, and plug-and-play Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Ethernet and modem cards.",
 desc:["The PAC Mate Omni is the Windows Mobile 6 generation of Freedom Scientific's PAC Mate. It keeps the earlier PAC Mate's shell, with a 400 MHz XScale processor, 64 MB of RAM and 128 MB of flash. The QWERTY QX400 measures 12.3 by 6.27 by 1.62 inches and weighs 2 pounds. Keys are on top, and ports and switches are on the back.","The QX400 keyboard has six rows. Escape, called the Close button, is first on the left of the top row, followed by F1 through F12. Three finger bumps between F4 and F5, F8 and F9, and F12 and Print Screen keep you oriented. The bottom row runs FN, Ctrl, Windows, Alt, Backslash, Space, Insert, Alt, Windows and Applications. The arrows are in the lower right, with Up above the other three. Delete, Home, Page Up, Page Down and End run down the right side.","On the back, from right to left, are the On/Off switch, the power jack, a mini A/B USB port, two CompactFlash slots, an infrared port, a 2.5 mm headset/microphone jack and a 3.5 mm headphone jack. On the top surface, the speaker is above F3 and the internal microphone is above Print Screen and Scroll Lock.","On the bottom, turned over with the back facing you, is a rectangular recess holding the serial number sticker. Just above it is a small round reset button you press with a paper clip or braille stylus. Across from it on the right is the release for the drawer or braille display. A 20- or 40-cell PAC Mate Portable Braille Display slides on and locks.","The lithium-polymer battery gives a warning at 30 percent and a critical alert at 12 percent; the flyer rates battery life at more than 20 hours. The braille BX models use the eight-key Perkins layout of the earlier BX400."]});
// Batch 19: Orbit Reader 20, Mantis Q40, Focus 40/14 Blue 5th gen, Canute 360, Chameleon 20
const OR20A="https://afb.org/aw/19/1/15167";
U(DEV,"or20",{s:{price:"$449 (APH, AccessWorld 2018)",dim:"About 6 × 4 × 1 in (AccessWorld)",disp:"20 cells, refreshed cell by cell",sto:"SD card (supplied inserted)",io:"Micro USB; Bluetooth"},src:[OR20A],
 ha:"Scott Davert's AccessWorld review (2018) credits Orbit Research and the Transforming Braille Group with holding the price to $449, distributed in the US by APH. It reaches that price by refreshing the cells one at a time rather than all at once. That causes a slight delay with external devices but almost none in the built-in reader, and the refresh is a bit noisy, 'no worse than a rather loud laptop.' The reader handles plain text and digital braille files only, and no case is included; Executive Products sold one for $78.95.",
 desc:["The Orbit Reader 20 is a low-cost 20-cell braille display and book reader from Orbit Research, sold in the US by APH. AccessWorld measured it at about 6 inches wide, 4 inches deep and just over 1 inch high.","With the braille display toward you, the 20 cells run along the front. At each end is a panning button that rocks up or down. Orientation dots sit above cells 5, 10 and 15. Behind the display, from left to right, are Dot 7, the space bar and Dot 8.","Directly behind the space bar are four arrow keys around a round Select button. Behind them is the six-key Perkins-style keyboard, with oval keys set straight across from each other.","On the back edge, from left to right, are the power button, an SD card slot with a card already inserted, and a Micro USB connector for charging and USB connection. On the front are two holes, one on each side, for a strap or lanyard, which is not supplied.","On the underside are many screws, four rubber feet and a plastic battery door held by two screws. It has no cursor-routing buttons. In standalone mode it offers a file manager, reader and editor; in remote mode it is a display for computers, phones and tablets over USB or Bluetooth."]});
const MQ="https://afb.org/aw/21/8/17143";
U(DEV,"mantis",{s:{price:"$2,495 (APH, AccessWorld 2020)",sto:"16 GB internal; SD up to 64 GB; USB-A flash drives",bat:"12 to 17 hours depending on Bluetooth and Wi-Fi use; about 1% per hour asleep",io:"USB-C, USB-A; up to 5 Bluetooth plus 1 USB"},src:[MQ,Q40],
 ha:"A collaboration between APH and HumanWare. Scott Davert reviewed it for AccessWorld in July 2020. He reported one crash before firmware 1.01 and none after it. He noted that switching devices meant pressing Home to leave the current connection, that updates could require re-pairing Bluetooth, and that start-up took about 15 seconds. He found the braille spongy, much like the BrailleNote Touch.",
 desc:["The Mantis Q40 is a 40-cell braille display with a full laptop-style QWERTY keyboard instead of braille keys, made by HumanWare with APH. It cost $2,495 in 2020.","With the display nearest you, the front edge has five thumb keys. From left to right they are Previous, Pan Left, Home (which also works as Escape), Pan Right and Next.","Along the left side, nearest you, is a USB-A port for flash drives, then a small round Power button, then the USB-C port for charging and connecting. Hold Power about two seconds to start; it takes about 15 seconds to boot, and a brief press puts it to sleep or wakes it. The back edge has an SD card slot.","On top, closest to you, are the 40 braille cells, each with a cursor-routing button behind it. Behind those is the QWERTY keyboard, laid out like a laptop without a number pad.","The bottom key row, from left to right, is Control, FN, Windows, Left Alt, Space, Right Alt, Right Control, then Left, Down and Right arrows, with Up slightly above Down. On a Mac or iOS device the left keys act as Control, FN, Option and Command. It has 16 GB of storage and downloads from Bookshare and NFB-NEWSLINE."]});
const F5="https://www.afb.org/aw/19/4/15103";
const FPH="The 5th generation is made of solid aluminum, with rubber bumpers on each side to absorb drops, which make it slightly longer than the 4th generation.";
U(DEV,"focus40b",{s:{price:"$2,995 (AccessWorld 2018)",bat:"About 20 hours on Bluetooth",io:"USB-C; microSD; 5 Bluetooth plus 1 USB"},src:[F5],
 ha:"Reviewed by Scott Davert in AccessWorld in April 2018. He wrote that early Focus displays used a serial cable, had audio jacks and only three front controls, and were meant for desktops. The 5th generation added a rugged body and a well-designed case. At launch its microSD slot could not yet be used, and VFO promised a spring firmware update for reading and writing BRF files.",
 desc:["The Focus 40 Blue 5th Generation is a 40-cell braille display from Freedom Scientific (VFO). "+FPH,"With the front controls nearest you, the front edge holds, from left to right: Left Panning button, Left Rocker bar, Left Selector, Left Shift, Right Shift, Right Selector, Right Rocker bar and Right Panning button. This is the same layout as the 4th generation.","On the left side, behind the left bumper and almost against it, is a microSD slot, then the Power button, with the USB-C port furthest back. The right side has nothing but the right bumper.","On top, closest to you, is a pronounced space bar. Behind it are the 40 braille cells, with a cursor-routing button behind each. At each end of the display is a Nav rocker bar with a Nav Mode button behind it.","Behind the cells is an eight-dot Perkins keyboard, with a Menu button between dots 1 and 4; Menu is the only new surface control. It connects to five Bluetooth devices and one USB device, runs about 20 hours on Bluetooth, and comes with a case."]});
U(DEV,"focus14b",{s:{price:"$1,295 (AccessWorld 2018)",bat:"About 20 hours on Bluetooth",io:"USB-C; microSD; 5 Bluetooth plus 1 USB"},src:[F5],
 ha:"Reviewed alongside the Focus 40 in AccessWorld (April 2018). Davert noted that the 4th-generation 14-cell display had been hard to get into its case, and that the new case fixed this.",
 desc:["The Focus 14 Blue 5th Generation is a 14-cell braille display from Freedom Scientific (VFO). "+FPH,"With the front controls nearest you, the front edge holds, from left to right: Left Selector, Left Rocker bar, Left Panning button, Left Shift, Right Shift, Right Panning button, Right Rocker bar and Right Selector. This order differs from the Focus 40.","On the left side, behind the left bumper, is a microSD slot, then the Power button, with the USB-C port furthest back. The right side has only the right bumper.","On top, closest to you, is the space bar. Behind it are the 14 braille cells with cursor-routing buttons behind them, and a Nav rocker bar with a Nav Mode button at each end.","Behind the cells is an eight-dot Perkins keyboard with a Menu button between dots 1 and 4. It runs about 20 hours on Bluetooth, connects to five Bluetooth devices plus USB, and cost $1,295 in 2018."]});
const CAN="https://afb.org/aw/23/6/17972";
U(DEV,"canute",{s:{price:"Under $2,900 (AccessWorld 2022)",disp:"9 lines × 40 six-dot cells (360 cells)",dim:"14.5 × 7.5 × 1.5 in (37 × 19 × 4 cm)",wt:"6 lb (2.8 kg)",bat:"None; mains power only",io:"HDMI, 3.5 mm jack (unused), SD, 2 USB-A, USB-B"},src:[CAN],
 ha:"Made by Bristol Braille Technology, a UK not-for-profit founded in 2011. After fourteen prototypes, the Canute 360 began shipping in 2020. Founder Ed Rogers named it after King Canute the Great, who was infamous for trying to hold back the ocean, because earlier multi-line displays had failed. AccessWorld noted that it takes about a minute to start and makes a quiet ticking sound while idle.",
 desc:["The Canute 360 is a multi-line braille e-reader from Bristol Braille Technology. It shows nine lines of 40 six-dot cells at once. It is 14.5 inches long, 7.5 inches deep and 1.5 inches high, weighs 6 pounds, and is meant to sit on a table. The case is aluminum with a plastic reading surface.","The nine lines are spaced further apart than in a braille book, like a double-spaced page. At the left end of each line is a braille line number and a triangular Line Select button. Above button 1 is a round Help button labeled H, and below button 9 is a square button labeled 0 for entering page numbers.","The front edge has three large rectangular buttons, with braille labels above them on the top: Back, Menu and Forward.","On the left side, from front to back, are an HDMI port that shows the braille on a monitor in a high-contrast braille font, and a 3.5 mm audio jack not yet used. About halfway back, at the very bottom of that side, is an SD card slot. Further back are two USB-A ports and a USB-B port for computers.","The back is mostly smooth. At its far right end is the power socket, with a spring-loaded on/off button just to its right. The right side has nothing. There is no battery, so it must be plugged in. It takes about a minute to start, refreshing the lines one at a time, and ticks quietly while idle."]});
const CH="https://nfb.org/blog/tech-review-chameleon-20-braille-display-aph";
U(DEV,"cham20",{s:{dim:"About 3.5 × 7 × 1 in (NFB review)",wt:"About 1 lb",io:"USB-C, USB-A, SD; Bluetooth"},src:[CH],
 ha:"APH's Chameleon 20 runs HumanWare-style software. NFB's review noted that the thumb keys 'operate exactly the same' as on HumanWare devices. At the time of the review its volume buttons and audio jack were not yet usable; APH planned full text-to-speech and audio playback later. Apps include an Editor, Terminal, Library, Calculator, File Manager, Date and Time, Settings and Online Services for Bookshare and NFB-NEWSLINE.",
 desc:["The Chameleon 20 is a 20-cell braille display and notetaker from APH. It measures roughly 3.5 by 7 by 1 inches and weighs about a pound.","The front edge has four thumb keys with a Home button in the middle. The outer two go to the previous and next item, the inner two pan the display left and right, and Home returns to the menu from any app.","The left edge has, from front to back, a standard USB-A port for flash drives, the power button and a USB-C port for charging or connecting. The only thing on the back edge is an SD card slot near the left. The right edge has two volume buttons and an audio jack.","On top, two space bars run along the front edge, with the 20 braille cells behind them and a cursor-routing key behind each cell. Near the back is the eight-key Perkins-style keyboard.","When it is charging while off, the display shows 'Charging' and then 'Charge Complete.' Hold the power button a few seconds to start; 'Starting' appears with two dots spinning as a progress indicator. Enter+P reports the battery, Enter+T the time and Enter+D the date."]});
// Batch 20: Miscellaneous - Note Teller 2 money identifier, Verbalise talking watches; Dot Watch 2 description
const NT2="https://www.afb.org/aw/8/3/14354",VERB="https://disabilitynow.wordpress.com/2016/11/21/product-review-verbalise-talking-watches-talking-the-talk-looking-the-look/",DW2="https://afb.org/aw/20/1/14990";
AC({id:"brytech",n:"BRYTECH",f:"Founding year not found in the AccessWorld article used",h:"Ottawa, Canada maker of the Note Teller money identifier (600 Peter Morand Crescent, per AccessWorld 2007). AccessWorld reported that as of January 1, 2007 the Note Teller 2 was the only portable device it knew of made for identifying U.S. paper money.",src:[NT2]});
AC({id:"verbalise",n:"Verbalise (The Talking Watch Shop)",f:"Founding year not found; the 2016 Disability Now review describes the range as new",h:"Brand set up by blind entrepreneur Martin Begg to make stylish talking watches, sold through his Talking Watch Shop (Disability Now, November 2016).",src:[VERB]});
AD({id:"noteteller2",n:"Note Teller 2 Money Identifier",co:"brytech",cat:M,yr:0,y:"On sale by January 2007 (AccessWorld, May 2007); launch year not stated",o:"A tabletop talking identifier for U.S. paper money, $1 to $100, in English or Spanish.",
 h:"AFB TECH evaluated it in May 2007, shortly after a federal judge ruled in December 2006 that U.S. currency was inaccessible to people who are visually impaired. Expert lab testers identified bills about 80% of the time; good-condition bills were easy and worn or wrinkled ones harder. $2 bills succeeded on only 6 of 36 tries (16.7%) and were called $50 five times. In the user study in Dallas, two $10 bills were announced as $100. AFB praised it as one of the easiest devices it had tested and its large-print and audio-CD manual. It found it too big for a pocket and questioned the $270 price.",
 s:{price:"$270 (AccessWorld, 2007)",dim:"6 × 3 × about 1 in",wt:"8 oz with battery",bat:"One 9-volt battery; says 'replace battery' when weak",out:"Male voice, English or Spanish; headphone jack"},
 f:["Recognizes U.S. bills $1 to $100; manufacturer-upgradable for new designs","Either end, either face up; insert 'swiftly'","Announces in 2 to 4 seconds; says 'cannot read' if unsure","Says 'out of order' when it needs repair","Manual in large print and on audio CD, English and Spanish"],
 src:[NT2],
 desc:["The Note Teller 2 from BRYTECH of Canada is a talking money identifier for U.S. paper currency. It is a black rectangular box 6 inches long, 3 inches wide and about 1 inch thick, weighing 8 ounces with its 9-volt battery.","On top, about two thirds of the way toward the back, is a slot for the bill. Molded edges along the sides guide the bill into it. Sliding the end of a bill in turns the unit on, and it does not matter which end or which face is up.","The front edge has a headphone jack and a slider switch. When the unit is silent, the slider sets the volume to low, medium or high; while it is speaking, the slider switches between English and Spanish.","The back edge is slightly thicker than the front and has a ring for a neck or wrist strap, or for securing the unit to a desk or counter. A vinyl sleeve protects it when not in use.","Within two to four seconds a clear male voice announces the denomination, or says 'cannot read.' It says 'replace battery' when the battery is weak and 'out of order' when it needs service. AFB found it very easy to use but too large for a pocket, and its accuracy was about 80% in lab tests."]});
AD({id:"verbaliserolx",n:"Verbalise Rol-X and Swarovski-crystal talking watches",co:"verbalise",cat:M,yr:2016,y:"2016 (reviewed as new additions in November 2016)",o:"Radio-controlled talking wristwatches designed to look stylish, with natural-sounding voices at a discreet volume.",
 h:"Disability Now editor Ian Macrae reviewed the range in November 2016. Martin Begg set out to fix two complaints about talking watches: that they look cheap and that their voices are cartoonish or so loud that everyone nearby hears. Macrae found the voices neutral and audible in average noise. He found the clasp and strap adjustment awkward at first. He wished the hourly announcements could be limited to daytime, since they had to be switched off at night.",
 s:{price:"£149.99, all models (2016)",models:"Men's Rol-X GB (gold and black) and SB (silver and black); women's Swv-855 gold, Swf-887 rose gold, Swf-863 silver",time:"Radio-controlled, set automatically from the atomic clock signal"},
 f:["Two voices: male (24-hour time) and female (12-hour time)","Optional hourly time announcement","Speech feedback when setting controls","Men's model has a 24-hour scale on the bezel"],
 src:[VERB],
 desc:["Verbalise talking watches are radio-controlled wristwatches from a company set up by blind entrepreneur Martin Begg. They were designed to look like ordinary fashion watches and to speak quietly in natural voices. All models cost £149.99 in 2016.","The men's watch is modeled on a 'Rol-X' design, in black and silver or black and gold. It has a decorative edge to the bezel, which also shows 24-hour time around the face. It comes on a leather strap with a clasp and can take most other straps.","The women's watch is decorated with Swarovski crystals and comes in gold, rose gold and silver.","Each watch offers two voices and two time formats: a male voice speaks 24-hour time and a female voice 12-hour time. Both have neutral British accents. The reviewer found them audible in average background noise without being loud. The watch can also announce the time every hour.","Because the watches are radio-controlled, they set themselves from the atomic clock signal. Other settings are easy, with speech feedback guiding you. The reviewer found the clasp fiddly at first and noted that the hourly announcement had to be switched off at night."]});
U(DEV,"dotwatch",{s:{price:"$399 (Dot Watch 2, AccessWorld January 2019)",dim:"43 mm round aluminum case",bat:"7 to 8 days (AccessWorld); magnetic USB charger",conn:"Bluetooth to Android and iOS"},src:[DW2],
 ha:"AccessWorld's January 2019 review states that the first-generation Dot Watch was discontinued in June 2018 and replaced by the Dot Watch 2. Scott Davert found battery life of seven to eight days. He noted that the default Tactile mode uses Dot's own time code, while Braille mode shows standard braille, and that notifications from a paired phone make it useful for deafblind users.",
 desc:["The Dot Watch 2 is a smartwatch from Dot Incorporated with a 4-cell refreshable braille display instead of a screen. It has a round aluminum 43-millimeter case, about the size of an Apple Watch though shaped differently, and cost $399 in 2019.","On the right side is a crown that turns either way, with a button on each side of it. Worn on the left wrist, the button nearer your hand is Home, which puts the watch in standby, and the other is Select, which wakes it. Holding Home for three seconds turns it on.","A protective cover over the face keeps debris out. On a slightly lower surface beneath it is the 4-cell braille display. Past a metal bar level with the cover is another bar, and behind that, parallel to cells 2 and 3, are two touch sensors: the one by cell 2 pans back and the one by cell 3 pans forward.","The band is a magnetic metal mesh, similar to Apple's Milanese Loop, and arrives already attached. Underneath the watch is a small indentation; the magnetic charger has three dots forming braille L, and lining the indentation up with them aligns the magnets. The watch vibrates when charging starts.","In Tactile mode, the default, the time uses Dot's own code: at 3:00, cell 1 is blank, cell 2 shows three pins, and each dot in cells 3 and 4 is five minutes. Braille mode uses ordinary braille, and the watch can be flipped for the right wrist. Even the box is tactile, with 'Dot Watch' embossed in braille."]});
// Batch 21: reword or fill the explanatory "not documented" sentences in descriptions
const RD=(id,o,n,src)=>{const d=DEV.find(x=>x.id===id);if(!d||!d.desc){console.log("RD missing",id);return;}let hit=false;d.desc=d.desc.map(p=>{if(p.includes(o)){hit=true;return p.replace(o,n);}return p;});if(!hit)console.log("RD no match",id,o.slice(0,40));if(src)d.src=(d.src||[]).concat(src.filter(u=>!(d.src||[]).includes(u)));};
const BNSM21="https://support.freedomscientific.com/Content/Documents/Manuals/Legacy/Braille_N_SpeakDocs/Braille_N_Speak_UserMan.txt",ETPL="https://cdn.thomasnet.com/ccp/01055195/75393.pdf",EXBS="https://www.extra.co.jp/product/bsonhand/",USA3="https://www.usatoday.com/story/tech/columnist/2017/02/15/high-tech-glasses-helping-blind-people-see/97960470/";
const BNSLAY="Blazie's Braille 'n Speak user manual (Freedom Scientific legacy edition, which refers to the 12-volt charger of later units) describes the layout this way. With the space bar toward you, the on/off rocker switch is about halfway back on the right side; rock it away from you for on. Just behind it is an earphone jack that can also feed an external speaker or a tape recorder. On the left side is an indented rectangular opening holding two identical round DIN serial ports, with the AC power and charger jack behind it.";
RD("bns","The exact port and switch layout of the 1987 classic is not documented in the sources found. Later models had a rocker power switch and earphone jack on the right side and serial ports on the left.",BNSLAY+" Whether the 1987 units matched this exactly is not stated.",[BNSM21]);
RD("bns","Its color is not documented in the sources found.","None of the sources checked for this entry mention its color.");
RD("bns640","The exact port layout is not documented in the sources found.",BNSLAY.replace("this way","for Braille 'n Speak units generally"),[BNSM21]);
RD("bns2000","Its color is not documented in the sources found.","None of the sources checked for this entry mention its color.");
RD("onhand","its weight is not documented in the sources found.","the Japanese distributor Extra lists it at 17.2 × 9.0 × 2.7 cm and 425 g (about 15 oz).",[EXBS]);
RD("es3","Battery life is not documented in the sources used.","The specifications in this entry give an integrated lithium-ion battery of about 6 hours.");
RD("es3","Its launch price is not documented in the sources used.","USA Today reported in February 2017 that it cost $9,995, down from the $15,000 of earlier eSight models, and that insurance did not cover it.",[USA3]);
RD("esgo","Its price is not documented in the sources used.","Its current list price is $4,950.");
RD("nueasy","Its price is not documented in the sources used.","Its listed price is $5,995.");
RD("romeo","Its launch price is not documented in the sources found.","Its launch price wasn't found; Enabling Technologies' price list effective July 1, 2012 shows the Romeo Attaché at $2,295 and the Attaché Pro at $2,495.",[ETPL]);
RD("romeopro50","Its launch price is not documented in the sources found.","Its launch price wasn't found; Enabling Technologies' price list effective July 1, 2012 shows it at $2,995, including ET Speaks and single-sheet tractors.",[ETPL]);
RD("es2","Its exact dimensions are not documented in the sources found.","The sources checked give no exact measurements for the headset.");
RD("blite40","Its exact size and weight are not documented in the sources found; the smaller","No source checked gives its own size and weight. For comparison, the smaller");
RD("bliteM","Exact dimensions and weight are not documented in the sources used.","The sources checked give no exact measurements or weight for either model.");
RD("bnmpqt","The exact key count and layout are not documented in the sources found.","The sources checked don't give a key-by-key layout.");
RD("bnapex18","Exact dimensions and weight for the 18-cell model are not documented in the sources found.","The sources checked give measurements only for the 32-cell Apex, not the 18-cell model.");
RD("dbc","Its exact size, weight and colors as a package are not documented separately from the mPower and the phone.","Its size and weight are those of the BrailleNote mPower and the phone it is built from; the sources give no separate figures for the combined package.");
RD("vsense","The exact placement of the jacks is not given in the sources found.","The sources checked don't say where on the case the jacks are.");
// Batch 22: descriptions (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const OR40R="https://www.helenkeller.org/a-review-of-the-orbit-reader-40/",OR40P="https://www.orbitresearch.com/products/blindness-products/braille-devices/orbit-reader-40/";
U(DEV,"or40",{s:{price:"$1,899 (Orbit Research, 2026); under $1,500 when reviewed in October 2021",dim:"11.6 × 3.8 × 1.3 in (29.5 × 9.6 × 3.3 cm)",wt:"1.65 lb (750 g)"},src:[OR40R,OR40P,"https://www.orbitresearch.com/support/orbit-reader-40-support/"],
ha:"Scott Davert of the Helen Keller National Center reviewed it in October 2021. Unlike the Orbit Reader 20, it added cursor-routing buttons, built-in braille translation and connections to up to five Bluetooth devices. It has no internal memory: files live on an SD card (4 to 32 GB) or a USB drive. He estimated battery life at 20 to 22 hours of heavy use and found the cells quieter than the Orbit Reader 20's but the keyboard louder. The user-replaceable battery cost $44.95 at the time and $49.95 by January 2023.",
desc:["The Orbit Reader 40 is a 40-cell braille display and note-taker from Orbit Research. It is a flat bar 11.6 inches long, 3.8 inches deep and 1.3 inches thick, weighing 1.65 pounds (750 grams). Orbit Research calls the construction solid and resistant to fluids and dust.","Place it with the Spacebar nearest you. Behind the Spacebar is the line of 40 cells, with a cursor-routing button behind each cell. At each end of the line is a rectangular rocker key that you press toward or away from you to pan.","Behind the routing buttons is a Perkins-style keyboard. From left to right are the keys for dots 7, 3, 2 and 1, then a four-way navigation pad with a Select button in its center, then dots 4, 5, 6 and 8. The front panel has two rectangular slots near each end for attaching a case or strap.","The left side has a USB-A port for thumb drives. The right side has, from front to back, the Power button, the USB-C port for charging and computer connection, and a 3.5 mm headphone jack. The SD card slot is on the back edge.","Underneath, each corner has a rubber foot. There are several screws, and a compartment for the user-replaceable battery. No case comes in the box. Orbit Research sold a padded nylon zip case with a top pocket for $59.95; a leather case was also offered."]});
const BRZ="https://afb.org/aw/10/2/16127",BRZ2="https://afb.org/aw/13/1/15891",BRZ3="https://www.avh.asso.fr/implantations/certam/evaluations-de-produits/trekker-breeze-navigateur-gps-de-humanware";
U(DEV,"breeze",{s:{price:"$929 including one regional map (AccessWorld 2012); €799 in France",dim:"129 × 60 × 29 mm",wt:"About 8 oz (AccessWorld); 240 g in its case (CERTAM)",bat:"Lithium-ion polymer, replaceable; about 8 hours, 5-hour charge"},src:[BRZ,BRZ2,BRZ3],
ha:"AccessWorld reviewed it in March 2009. HumanWare had heard from users and mobility instructors that existing GPS products were too complicated, and that many Trekker owners only used Where Am I and their own recorded landmarks. So the Breeze has 9 buttons instead of the Trekker's 39 and no keypad for typing destinations: you must visit and record a landmark before it can guide you there. Leader Dogs for the Blind helped shape it. It finds satellites in about two to three minutes and describes intersections about 30 feet ahead.",
desc:["The Trekker Breeze is a handheld talking GPS from HumanWare. AccessWorld compared it to a chubby TV remote. It is 129 by 60 by 29 millimeters and weighs about 8 ounces. It came in a leather case with a belt clip, a shoulder strap, and a small external speaker with a clip.","The face has nine large buttons in three rows of three, each a different shape and color and easy to tell apart by touch. Above them, at the top left, is a microphone for recording landmark names, and at the top right is the built-in speaker.","The top row has Information, then a large orange circular Where Am I button in the middle, then Repeat. The middle row has the Left arrow, Confirm and the Right arrow. The bottom row has Explore, Record and Go To.","The left side has a volume thumb wheel, the SD card slot and the USB connector, under a protective flap. The right side has the power switch and a Reset button, and the power connector at the lower right under a rubber flap. The jack for the external speaker is in the middle of the rounded top edge.","The battery compartment is on the back. A lanyard attaches at the base. It beeps once at power-on and twice at power-off, and says \"Initializing Breeze\" with the battery level."]});
const BEZ="https://lssproducts.com/content/251076.pdf",BEZ2="https://www.sightandsound.co.uk/a-review-of-the-hims-blaze-ez-by-jackie-brown/",BEZ3="https://www.bapingroup.com/blaze-ez.html";
U(DEV,"blazeez",{s:{dim:"116.9 × 59.3 × 16.4 mm (manual); 4.59 × 2.31 × 0.64 in",wt:"118 g (manual); 138 g (Sight and Sound review)",sto:"16 GB (about 12 GB free)",io:"Micro-USB, SD slot, headphone, line-in, Wi-Fi b/g/n, Bluetooth",price:"$595 (BAPIN Group listing)"},src:[BEZ,BEZ2,BEZ3],
ha:"Model T70EZ. Similar in size to its predecessor, the BookSense, but with fewer keys, it added a built-in camera for one-button OCR. It plays DAISY, NLS, Bookshare and Learning Ally books, music and podcasts, and has an FM radio and recorder. Jackie Brown's review for Sight and Sound found a 4.5-hour charge gave about 17 hours of use, liked the sound, but missed a phone-style keypad like the Victor Reader Stream's.",
desc:["The Blaze EZ is a pocket book player and reader from HIMS, about the size and shape of a smartphone. Its manual gives it as 59.3 millimeters wide, 116.9 long and 16.4 thick, and 118 grams; a reviewer weighed it at 138 grams. The case is white, with contrasting color on the Power, Media, Radio and Books buttons.","Hold it keypad up. At the top center is a slightly recessed square Power button, with a stereo speaker grille on each side. Below and to the left is a round Record button. Below that is a row of three square buttons with braille letters: M for Music or Media, R for Radio and B for Books.","Below them is a circular navigation pad of Up, Down, Left and Right with a small round Select button in the middle. Below that are three buttons: a square Cancel on the left, a small dot-like Explorer button in the middle, and a square OCR button marked with braille O on the right. At the very bottom are two status lights on the left and the microphone on the right.","The left side has the SD card slot in its lower part. The right side has a rotary volume control, a control for speed and voice guidance, and a lock key. The bottom edge has the headphone socket, the micro-USB charging port and a line-in socket. A small hole in the center of the top edge takes the lanyard.","On the back, near the top, is the camera lens. A removable cover on the back hides the flat rectangular battery. An accessory pack added a stand for OCR, a spare battery and charger, a remote and a charging cradle."]});
const MON="https://www.aph.org/product/monarch/",MON2="https://nfb.org/resources/publications-and-media/access-on-podcast/hands-monarch-dynamic-tactile-multi-line-braille",MON3="https://nfb.org/images/nfb/publications/fr/fr43/4/fr430409.htm",MON4="https://www.aph.org/blog/making-the-monarch-a-revolutionary-device-for-all/";
U(DEV,"monarch",{y:"Shipping from September 17, 2024",s:{bat:"Over 24 hours (APH); 8 hours planned in the early spec sheet"},src:[MON,MON2,MON3,MON4],
ha:"APH partnered with HumanWare just over four years before launch; HumanWare designed the display around the braille cell from the South Korean company Dot, and the NFB took part in development. APH began shipping it to students on September 17, 2024. Apps include KeyWord, a braille editor for BRF and eBRF files, the Tactile Viewer linked to APH's Tactile Graphics Image Library of over two thousand graphics, KeyMath, a graphing calculator built with Desmos, and Victor Reader for Bookshare and NFB-NEWSLINE. It carries a two-year warranty and is available with federal quota funds. The NFB's reviewer noted you must lift your hands as it refreshes, and that refreshing is noisy.",
desc:["The Monarch is a multi-line braille and tactile graphics device from APH and HumanWare. It is about the size of a 15-inch gaming laptop: 15.9 inches wide, 10.5 inches deep and 1.4 inches high, weighing about 4.65 pounds (2.1 kilograms). The NFB calls it a workstation rather than a portable note-taker. It ships in a protective bubble sleeve with a hard carrying case.","Most of the top is the tactile surface: a continuous grid of 96 by 40 evenly spaced pins, 3,840 in all. It shows ten lines of 32 braille cells, or a raised picture across the whole surface. Because the pins are evenly spaced, line spacing can be widened for beginning readers. A user-replaceable membrane protects it from dust and spills.","Nearest you is an eight-key Perkins-style braille keyboard. APH lists up and down arrows, direction pads, Android navigation keys and controls for paging and zooming. Infrared sensors detect touch gestures on the surface, and a vibration motor gives haptic feedback.","For connections, it has a USB-C port, a USB-A port, an HDMI port that shows a sighted teacher or classmate what is on the display, and an audio jack. It also has a microphone, stereo speakers, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.","When the display refreshes, many pins move at once with an audible clatter, and you must lift your hands for it to refresh correctly. A double press of the refresh button redraws it."]});
const VU="https://nfb.org/blog/varioultra-overview",VU2="https://afb.org/aw/18/4/15220";
U(DEV,"vario",{y:"2014 (NFB overview published February 2, 2015)",s:{price:"VarioUltra 20: $2,395; VarioUltra 40: $3,995 (AccessWorld 2017)",dim:"VU20: 7.4 × 3.5 × 0.7 in; VU40: 12.4 × 3.5 × 0.7 in",wt:"VU20: 0.7 lb; VU40: 1.1 lb"},src:[VU,VU2],
ha:"Made by BAUM Retec AG in 20- and 40-cell sizes. Built-in apps include a word processor, PDF and spreadsheet viewers, a calculator, timers and an alarm clock. It has no SD slot; files move over USB, with the unit appearing as a drive on a PC. AccessWorld compared the VarioUltra 40 with the HIMS Braille Edge 40 ($2,795) in 2017, finding the VarioUltra lighter and slimmer but over $1,000 dearer.",
desc:["The VarioUltra is a very thin braille display and basic note-taker from BAUM, in 20- and 40-cell sizes. The 20-cell model is 7.4 by 3.5 by 0.7 inches and weighs 0.7 pounds; the 40-cell model is 12.4 inches long and weighs 1.1 pounds. Reviewers describe it as slim and well built.","The front of the top is sloped at about 30 degrees. On that slope, from left to right, are system keys S1 and S2, a spacebar, a round joystick called the NaviStick, a second spacebar, and system keys S3 and S4. The NaviStick also presses down to select.","Behind these is the braille display, with three buttons stacked in a vertical line at each end for panning and other functions. Behind the display are the cursor-routing keys, and at the back is a standard eight-key braille keyboard.","The right side has only the power button, just behind the front edge. The left side has, from front to back, a keyboard lock switch, a switch between note-taker and display modes, a micro-USB port and a full-size USB port. The back edge is empty. The two front corners have attachment points for a shoulder strap.","It came in a leather case that closes with magnets and lets you use the display while carrying it by the strap."]});
const RB="https://afb.org/aw/11/3/16054";
U(DEV,"refresh18",{s:{price:"$1,695 (AccessWorld, July 2010)",bat:"About 30 hours on Bluetooth, up to 100 on USB; about 2-hour charge; not user-replaceable"},src:[RB],
ha:"Deborah Kendrick reviewed it for AccessWorld in July 2010. It works as both a braille display and a braille keyboard. It shipped with a USB cable, a CD of documentation and drivers, and quick-start sheets in print and braille, but no AC adapter. When powered off, all dots stand up; a press of about two seconds on Power vibrates it and shows APH and the serial number.",
desc:["The Refreshabraille 18 is APH's 18-cell Bluetooth braille display and keyboard. Its footprint is about the size of an index card, roughly 3 by 5 inches, and it weighs about 11 ounces. It is housed in a sturdy metal case.","Hold it with the display furthest from you. There are 18 eight-dot piezoelectric cells, with a cursor-routing button below each. Below those are three evenly spaced rectangular buttons.","Next is a straight row of six braille keys. Centered below them, where a spacebar might be, is a round joystick, and directly below that is the spacebar itself. On either side of the spacebar is a key for dot 7 or dot 8. Because the unit is narrow, these sit below the other keys and are pressed with the thumbs.","The power button is centered on the front edge, flush with the case, so the outline stays smooth.","The mini-USB port is in a cut-out on the bottom, so a plugged-in cable lies under the display, protected."]});
const B40X="https://afb.org/aw/22/7/17621";
U(DEV,"bi40x",{s:{price:"$3,195 (BI 20X: $1,895), per AccessWorld 2021"},src:[B40X],
ha:"AccessWorld called it a new class of smart braille display. It can connect to Wi-Fi and log into NLS BARD, and its KeyPad editor is meant for quick notes: press Power and you can type within about five seconds. The reviewer missed a spellchecker. The smaller BI 20X has Bluetooth 4.0 instead of 5.0 and an SD slot instead of a USB storage port.",
desc:["The Brailliant BI 40X is a 40-cell braille display from HumanWare with simple built-in apps. It weighs about 690 grams.","On the top surface, from back to front, are eight braille keys for dots 1 to 8, then 40 cursor-routing buttons, then the 40 braille cells. At each end of the cells are three command keys arranged like a braille cell. In front of the cells are two spacebars, one under each thumb.","The front edge has four thumb keys for navigation, with a round Home/Select button in the center.","The left edge has, from back to front, a USB-C port for a computer or charger, the Power button, and a USB-A port for flash drives. The right edge has an earphone jack and volume up and down buttons.","The top also has built-in stereo speakers. At the 2021 review they only gave system beeps, though HumanWare planned to add audio."]});
const EX8="https://www.avh.asso.fr/implantations/certam/evaluations-de-produits/loupe-electronique-portable-explore-8-dhumanware",EX8B="https://www.maxiaids.com/product/humanware-explore-8-inch-handheld-electronic-video-magnifier";
U(DEV,"explore8",{s:{dim:"204 × 153.2 × 24 mm (8 × 6 × 0.94 in)",wt:"580 g (1.3 lb)",bat:"Non-removable 6,700 mAh lithium polymer, 4.5 hours; 3.5-hour charge",io:"USB-C, HDMI"},src:[EX8,EX8B],
ha:"Its two 21-megapixel cameras cover close work from 8.5 to 15 cm and distance from 15 cm to infinity. It offers 17 color schemes and auto-shutoff after 1, 2, 5 or 10 minutes. Its LED lights turn off when it is lifted. It is for low vision, with no speech output.",
desc:["The explorē 8 is an 8-inch handheld video magnifier from HumanWare. It is 8 inches wide, 6 inches tall and just under an inch thick, and weighs 580 grams, about 1.3 pounds. It has a rubberized grip.","The front is almost entirely an 8-inch touchscreen. Below it is a control panel with three large physical buttons: Zoom minus on the left, a contrast or color button in the middle, and Zoom plus on the right. The zoom buttons change magnification in half steps, or continuously when held.","The left side has, from the top, the power button, an HDMI port for a TV and a USB-C port for charging and a computer. On the top edge, at the right, is a capture button: a press freezes the image, and a long press saves it.","On the back, at the top center, are two cameras, one for close-up reading and one for distance, flanked by two rows of three LED lights.","Along the bottom of the back is a solid alloy folding stand the full width of the device. Opened, it tilts the screen about 30 degrees toward you on a table and switches to the close-up camera."]});
// Batch 23: descriptions and fills (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const SPN="https://nfb.org/blog/access-tech-review-hims-sense-player",SPH="https://himsintl.com/en/daisyplayer/view.php?idx=12";
U(DEV,"senseplayer",{s:{dim:"About 64 × 130 × 14.5 mm",wt:"About 140 g",bat:"Li-polymer 3.8 V, 2,300 mAh (8.74 Wh); removable",io:"USB-C, 4-pole earphone jack, microSD, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth",cam:"13 MP with LED flash (OCR model)"},src:[SPN,SPH],
ha:"Sold in a standard version and a slightly dearer OCR version with a camera. It plays music, DAISY books, documents, podcasts and internet radio, and can install a limited selection of Android apps such as Audible, BARD Mobile and Netflix. Its Smart Connect mode turns it into a Bluetooth keypad for an iPhone or Android phone, for navigating, typing, calling and media control. The NFB's review found the OCR good but not better than phone apps such as Seeing AI or Lookout, and found Smart Connect sometimes slow to reconnect.",
desc:["The SensePlayer is a thin rectangular book and media player from HIMS, about 64 by 130 millimeters and 14.5 millimeters thick, weighing about 140 grams.","Most of the front is a 12-key telephone-style keypad. Above it is a ring of four direction buttons with a confirm button in the center, and four more function keys around them. Stereo speakers run along the top and bottom edges of the front.","The top row of keys has two distinct clicks: a short press, and a deeper press you can also hold. These keys switch between modes, manage Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, and open Smart Connect. A short press usually announces a status, a deep press toggles it, and a long press opens its settings.","The left edge has a Record button at the top, then a mode button, two volume buttons and a microSD slot. The mode button changes what the volume buttons adjust, such as voice speed or the music equalizer. The right edge has the power button and a key-lock switch. The bottom edge has the headphone jack on the left and the USB-C port on the right.","On the back is a lanyard loop at the top. On the OCR version, the camera is directly below the loop. The rest of the back is the cover over the removable battery."]});
const IB1="https://afb.org/aw/13/12/15862",IB2="https://afb.org/aw/16/6/15449",IB3="https://www.orbitresearch.com/orbit-research-introduces-ibill-talking-bank-note-reader/";
U(DEV,"ibill",{yr:2009,y:"Announced December 19, 2009; second generation reviewed December 2012",s:{wt:"1.5 oz (Orbit Research, 2009)",price:"$99 at launch (December 2009); $119 for the second generation (AccessWorld 2012); free through the BEP"},src:[IB1,IB2,IB3],
ha:"Orbit Research, of Wilmington, Delaware, launched it on December 19, 2009 at $99, about a third of the cost of existing readers, claiming better than 99.9 percent accuracy in under a second. AccessWorld reviewed the first model in May 2010. The second generation, reviewed in December 2012, kept the size but replaced the closed slot with an open guide, recessed the buttons, and added a key ring and a 2.5 mm headphone jack. In vibration mode, $1, $2 and $5 are one, two and three short pulses; $10, $20 and $50 are one, two and three long pulses; $100 is short-long-short-long. Tone mode uses low and high beeps in the same pattern.",
desc:["The iBill is a small black talking money identifier from Orbit Research, about the size of a pack of chewing gum: 3 inches long, 1.6 inches wide and 0.7 inch thick, weighing about 1.5 ounces. It is made of rugged plastic.","On the second-generation model, one large face has an overlapping plastic guide fixed on top, offset slightly so it does not reach the right or top edges. This leaves an open platform where you slide in the narrow end of a bill.","There is a button at each end, either of which starts identification. Each is textured, marked by a raised box with a vertical bar, and slightly recessed so it is not pressed by accident in a pocket. Holding one and tapping the other cycles through vibration, tone and three speech volumes.","A tiny metal key ring is on one end. Almost at the corner beside it is a 2.5 mm headphone jack, which AccessWorld found very hard to find by touch until you know where it is.","The battery compartment for one AAA battery is on the bottom, marked by two raised ridges; the lid comes off by pressing down and pulling forward. The battery lasts about a year. When it runs low, two short beeps or three very short pulses follow each result."]});
const DPN="https://nfb.org/images/nfb/publications/bm/bm26/bm2605/bm260506.htm",DPD="https://www.dotincorp.com/en/product/pad",DPR="https://shop.rnib.org.uk/products/dot-pad";
U(DEV,"dotpad",{s:{dim:"273.5 × 228.5 × 31 mm (Dot Pad, Dot Inc.); 27.3 × 22.8 × 1.6 cm (Dot Pad X, RNIB)",wt:"1.2 kg (Dot Pad); 1.4 kg (Dot Pad X, RNIB); about 2.5 lb (NFB)",disp:"300 eight-pin cells (30 × 10) = 2,400 pins, plus a 20-cell braille line",bat:"10,000 mAh 3.7 V; 8 to 11 hours",cpu:"ARM Cortex-M4 32-bit, 120 MHz (Dot Pad X)",io:"Bluetooth LE 5.0; two USB-C ports (Dot Pad X)",price:"Dot Pad: $10,000 to $12,000 range (Dot Inc.); Dot Pad X: about $6,000 (NFB, May 2026); £6,495 ex VAT (RNIB)"},src:[DPN,DPD,DPR],
ha:"Made by Dot Inc. of South Korea, whose cell technology is also used in the Monarch. The Dot Pad X uses a newer generation of the cell that refreshes faster. It is a display, not a computer: it has no operating system, document storage, keyboard or cursor routing, and relies on an iPhone, iPad or Windows PC running VoiceOver, NVDA or JAWS. Dot Inc. offers Dot Book for reading up to ten lines of six-dot braille, and the Dot Cloud graphics library. The NFB found its battery outlasted the stated eight hours.",
desc:["The Dot Pad is a tactile graphics display from Dot Inc. The NFB compares the Dot Pad X to an iPad Pro in size: under an inch thick and about 2.5 pounds. Dot Inc. gives the original Dot Pad as 273.5 by 228.5 by 31 millimeters and 1.2 kilograms.","Most of the top is the graphics area: 300 eight-pin cells, 30 across by 10 down, making 2,400 pins. It can show a raised picture, or multi-line braille at 20 cells per line. A protective membrane covers the pins and gives them a distinctive feel.","It has six tactile buttons that can be assigned to functions. They move forward and back through text and invert, zoom, rotate or pan images.","Below the graphics area, and below these controls, is a separate single line of 20 braille cells for text. You can read text there with one hand while exploring a graphic with the other.","The Dot Pad X has two USB-C ports for charging and data. A blue light shows a Bluetooth connection and a red light shows charging. The cells will not refresh while a finger rests on them, so lift your hands briefly to let the picture change."]});
const OSN="https://nfb.org/resources/publications-and-media/access-podcast/comprehensive-review-and-demonstration-orbit-speak",OSP="https://www.orbitresearch.com/products/blindness-products/braille-devices/orbit-speak/";
U(DEV,"orbspeak",{y:"Announced at CSUN, March 2022",s:{price:"Orbit Speak $599; Orbit Speak Plus $749",dim:"6.3 × 2.6 × 0.6 in (Orbit Research); 6.7 × 2.9 × 0.6 in (NFB review)",wt:"5 oz / 180 g (Orbit Research); 7.4 oz / 210 g (NFB review)",sto:"32 GB, plus SD up to 2 TB",bat:"About 15 hours of continuous playback at full volume",tts:"RUNA TTS (Vocalizer voices; Zoe by default in the US), or Google TTS"},src:[OSN,OSP],
ha:"Jonathan Mosen's review for the NFB's Access On podcast notes that it answered calls for a modern Braille 'n Speak and predates the return of the Blazie brand. It is braille in and speech out, with no braille display. The standard and Plus models are physically identical. The Plus adds a music player and more, and upgrading later costs $50 more than buying the Plus at first. Its menus use chords from as far back as the VersaBraille of the 1980s.",
desc:["The Orbit Speak is a pocket note-taker from Orbit Research, with a braille keyboard and speech but no braille display. It is a small bar about 6.5 inches long, under 3 inches wide and 0.6 inch thick, about the weight of a smartphone. Orbit Research gives 180 grams; the NFB's review gives 210 grams.","Place it on a desk. At the back of the top surface is a six-key Perkins-style keyboard with quiet scissor keys. Between dots 1 and 4 is a navigation pad of up, down, left and right arrows around a Select button.","Directly in front of the pad is the spacebar. Immediately left and right of the spacebar are the keys for dots 7 and 8, used for Backspace and Enter and pressed with the thumbs, which keeps the unit narrow. Beyond them are function keys: F1 and F2 on the left, F3 and F4 on the right.","The left side has a 3.5 mm headphone jack and the power button, which has a distinctive raised nub. Holding it from a full shutdown gives a series of vibrations; a tap puts it into hibernation and a second tap wakes it where you left off. The right side has a USB-C port for charging and accessories, including USB audio devices.","The SD card slot is on the front edge. It has a built-in speaker and vibration feedback."]});
const BET="https://www.avh.asso.fr/implantations/certam/evaluations-de-produits/lecteur-multimedias-machine-lire-de-poche-blaze-et";
U(DEV,"blazeet",{s:{dim:"116 × 58 × 16 mm",wt:"136 g with battery",bat:"Lithium polymer 3.7 V, 2,050 mAh, removable; 13 hours; 3-hour charge (4 from a computer); spare battery included",cam:"5 MP",price:"€990; €1,180 with the Premium pack (France, 2016)",tts:"Acapela"},src:[BET],
ha:"Made by the Korean company HIMS in partnership with Visiole. The French CERTAM evaluation (published December 16, 2016) gives a French release date of February 2016. It differs from the Blaze EZ mainly by its number keypad, which adds navigation and text entry. It has 28 keys in all. Features include DAISY and media playback, podcasts and web radio, recording in MP3, WAV or DAISY, an FM radio, OCR with the camera, color detection, a calculator and alarms.",
desc:["The Blaze ET is a pocket book player and reader from HIMS, close to the size of a mobile phone: 116 by 58 by 16 millimeters, and 136 grams with its battery. It has no screen; small lights show battery, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth status.","At the top of the front are grilles over the stereo speakers, with the Power button between them. A long press of about four seconds turns it on or off, and a short press gives the date and time. Below are three keys, left to right: Info, Home and OCR.","In the middle is a five-key pad of four arrows around an OK key. Just below are three keys: Cancel, Menu and Review, which repeats the last announcement. The lower part of the front is a number keypad. A long press of the star key starts a key describer.","The left side has, from top to bottom, a voice-control key that chooses what to adjust, plus and minus keys, and the SD card slot. The right side has the Record key, a key-lock slider and a reset hole. The bottom edge has, from left to right, a 3.5 mm headphone jack, a micro-USB port for charging and file transfer, and a 3.5 mm jack for an external microphone or line-in.","The top edge has the lanyard attachment. On the back, the camera lens is in the middle near the top, and the battery compartment covers about three quarters of the back."]});
const SL5="https://www.orbitresearch.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/Orbit-Slate-520-Brochure-v0.4.pdf",SLJ="https://pianojuan.com/review-resena-orbit-slate-520-el-siguiente-nivel-en-braille-electronico-y-portatil/";
U(DEV,"slate",{s:{disp:"Slate 520: 5 lines × 20 cells (100 cells); Slate 340: 3 lines × 40 cells",dim:"Slate 520: 16.6 × 29.0 × 3.3 cm (6.3 × 11.4 × 1.3 in)",wt:"Slate 520: 1,430 g (3.15 lb)",io:"USB-C, USB-A, full-size SD card; Bluetooth (up to 5 hosts)",price:"Slate 520 $3,495 and Slate 340 $3,995 including shipping (June 2023 purchase, per Juan Pablo's review)"},src:[SL5,SLJ],
ha:"Orbit Research's brochure calls the Slate 520 the world's first multi-line braille display. It suggests uses such as spatial math with fractions and matrices, braille music and spreadsheets. It offers 2D cursor routing, onboard translation in over 40 languages, a book reader, a note-taker and clock, calendar and calculator apps. A Spanish-language review from February 2024 reports that early units shipped late because of component quality problems.",
desc:["The Orbit Slate 520 is a five-line, 20-cell braille display from Orbit Research; the Slate 340 has three lines of 40 cells. The Slate 520 is about the length of a letter-size sheet but about 2 inches narrower. It is 1.3 inches thick, like a closed thin laptop, weighs 3.15 pounds, and is made of very smooth plastic.","From the back, the top has a row of cursor-routing buttons, then a braille line, then a separator, then the next braille line, and so on. The separators carry raised vertical lines, with every fifth line more prominent, to help you count across.","At each end of each braille line is a pair of rocker buttons for scrolling. The top line's buttons move one line at a time; the lower ones move further, up to five lines at once.","Near the front is a round direction pad with a Select button in the middle, and a Perkins-style keyboard with oval keys for dots 7 and 8 beside the spacebar, used as Backspace and Enter.","The left edge has a small square power button. The SD card slot is in the middle of an edge toward the right corner. The right edge has the USB-C port for computer connection and charging, and a USB-A port for flash drives. It beeps and vibrates to confirm actions, and came with a padded zip cloth case with a front pocket."]});
U(DEV,"monarch",{s:{price:"About $15,000, varying by region and channel (NFB Braille Monitor, May 2026)"},src:[DPN]});
const GPR="https://sites.aph.org/pr/aph-and-orbit-research-announce-the-worlds-first-affordable-refreshable-tactile-graphics-display/";
U(DEV,"graphiti",{y:"Announced August 2, 2016 (APH and Orbit Research)",src:[GPR],
ha:"APH and Orbit Research announced it on August 2, 2016 as the world's first affordable refreshable tactile graphics display, built on Orbit's Tactuator technology. Each of its 2,400 pins, in a 60 by 40 array, can rise to different heights to represent topographic maps and shades of gray or color, and can blink individually. It connects by USB-C to computers, tablets, phones or the Orion TI-84 Plus talking graphing calculator, and has an HDMI port that makes it appear as a monitor. It also has a cursor pad, an SD card slot for standalone use, and a touch interface where tracing a shape with a fingertip raises the pins along the path."});
// Batch 24: Miscellaneous money identifiers (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const CMR="https://afb.org/aw/21/7/17116",BEPA="https://www.bep.gov/services/currency-accessibility/mobile-applications/",CRA="https://cashreader.app/about/",LTE="https://www.engadget.com/2011-03-10-iphone-app-helps-the-blind-id-currency.html",LTF="https://www.freelists.org/post/real-eyes/Ipplex-Releases-LookTel-Money-Reader-for-iOS-Devices",FBR="https://archive.nytimes.com/gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/02/19/for-the-visually-impaired-money-talks/";
AC({id:"bep",n:"U.S. Bureau of Engraving and Printing (BEP)",f:"Founding year not covered by the pages used here",h:"The U.S. Treasury bureau that prints Federal Reserve notes. Until redesigned currency with tactile features arrives, it gives eligible blind and visually impaired people a free iBill reader (AccessWorld, June 2015), and it publishes the free EyeNote app.",src:[BEPA,"https://afb.org/aw/16/6/15449"]});
AC({id:"cashreader",n:"Cash Reader s.r.o.",f:"2018, Brno, Czech Republic",h:"A Czech assistive-technology team led by founder and CEO Tomas Jelinek (iOS), with co-founder Martin Doudera (Android). It began in 2018 because no app could recognize the Czech koruna. Its recognition technology is also licensed to smart glasses, specialized phones and digital magnifiers. Awards include the Zero Project Award 2022.",src:[CRA]});
AC({id:"ipplex",n:"IPPLEX (LookTel)",f:"Founding year not covered by the pages used here",h:"Maker of the LookTel recognition apps for people with low vision or blindness, including LookTel Money Reader (March 2011). According to AccessWorld (2020), NantMobile Health bought Money Reader in 2016 and moved it to a subscription.",src:[LTF,CMR]});
AC({id:"franklin",n:"Franklin Electronic Publishers",f:"Founding year not covered by the page used here",h:"In 2010 it marketed the Franklin Bill Reader, a talking currency identifier (New York Times, February 2010).",src:[FBR]});
AD({id:"eyenote",n:"EyeNote (BEP money identifier app)",co:"bep",cat:M,yr:2011,y:"2011 (iOS); EyeNote 4.0 on August 16, 2024; later released for Android",o:"The U.S. government's free app that names the denomination of U.S. Federal Reserve notes from Series 1996 onward, using the phone camera.",
 h:"The BEP released EyeNote for iOS in 2011. Version 4.0 (August 16, 2024) is 64-bit and needs iOS 11 or later, covering the iPhone 6 and newer. The Android version needs Android 11 and at least 4 GB of RAM. It scans continuously, so there is no need to hold still or take a photo. It reads the face or back in any orientation, even part of a note, works offline, and speaks English or Spanish to match the device. It doesn't check whether a note is genuine. In AccessWorld's July 2020 test on an iPhone SE, it named every U.S. bill within a second and showed front or back on a braille display. It gave no ready prompt, though, and spoke in the Samantha voice rather than the user's VoiceOver voice. The BEP's older IDEAL Currency Identifier for Android has been withdrawn.",
 s:{price:"Free",plat:"iOS 11 or later; Android 11 or later with 4 GB RAM",out:"Speech through VoiceOver; braille on a connected display",lang:"English, Spanish"},
 f:["U.S. notes only, Series 1996 to present","Continuous scanning, no photo needed","Works offline","Either side, any orientation, partial notes","Does not detect counterfeits"],src:[BEPA,CMR]});
AD({id:"cashreaderapp",n:"Cash Reader",co:"cashreader",cat:M,yr:2018,y:"Started 2018",o:"A banknote-recognition app for iOS and Android that works offline and covers nearly every currency in the world.",
 h:"Built in Brno because no app could read the Czech koruna. It grew from one currency to ten, then fifty, and its makers say it now covers nearly every banknote, trained on over 10 million photos. AccessWorld's July 2020 review found it supporting nearly 100 currencies, with a 14-day trial, then $0.99 a month, $5.99 a year or $17.99 for life. It offered Siri shortcuts for each currency, optional haptic patterns for each denomination, an automatic flashlight in the dark, low-vision contrast settings and a choice of front or back camera.",
 s:{price:"Free trial, then subscription ($0.99/month, $5.99/year or $17.99 lifetime in July 2020)",plat:"iOS, Android",out:"Speech, haptics, beeps"},src:[CRA,CMR]});
AD({id:"looktelmr",n:"LookTel Money Reader",co:"ipplex",cat:M,yr:2011,y:"March 8, 2011 (iOS)",o:"An early iPhone app that named U.S. bills from $1 to $100 in real time through the camera, without an internet connection.",
 h:"IPPLEX released it on March 8, 2011 for $1.99, for the iPhone 4, iPhone 3GS and fourth-generation iPod touch. It used LookTel's patented object recognition on a continuous video stream, so you didn't need to hold still or take a photo, and it spoke results in a clear female voice. AccessWorld's Scott Davert called it unequivocally a top performer. In 2016 NantMobile Health bought it and moved it to a subscription; by 2020 the version he had bought in 2011 was no longer available.",
 s:{price:"$1.99 at launch (2011)",plat:"iPhone 4 / 3GS, 4th-generation iPod touch (2011)",out:"Female voice"},src:[LTF,LTE,CMR]});
AD({id:"franklinbill",n:"Franklin Bill Reader",co:"franklin",cat:M,yr:2010,y:"On the market by February 2010",o:"A handheld talking identifier for U.S. paper money from $1 to $100, speaking English or Spanish.",
 h:"The New York Times described it in February 2010 as a $300 device using object recognition to name a bill's denomination aloud. It recognized current and recent series, and new designs could be downloaded into it from Franklin's website using a Windows PC. Results came through a built-in speaker or wired earbuds. The article placed it in the context of a 2008 court ruling that the U.S. government discriminated against blind people by not making bills distinguishable.",
 s:{price:"$300 (2010)",out:"Speech, English or Spanish; speaker or earbuds"},src:[FBR]});
// Batch 25: DOS/OS2 screen reader histories (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const VEA="https://afb.org/aw/3/1/14962",JDF="https://msa.maryland.gov/megafile/msa/speccol/sc5300/sc5339/000113/000000/000447/unrestricted/20040917e/4-art.html",THI="https://afb.org/blindness-and-low-vision/using-technology/interviews-technology-pioneers/ted-henter",SR2A="https://sharktastica.co.uk/resources/docs/txt/IBM_292-363_SR2_1992_IBM.txt",RDC95="https://www.duxburysystems.org/downloads/library/news/new95.htm";
U(DEV,"vocaleyes",{ha:"Doug Geoffray began writing Vocal-Eyes in 1988 at Computer Aids Corporation (CAC). It was one of the first DOS screen readers for PCs, and it was nearly finished when CAC went out of business in November 1989. Geoffray and Dan Weirich then founded GW Micro in February 1990. Vocal-Eyes, the Sounding Board speech synthesizer and support for older CAC talking products were the company's main products in its early years. GW Micro's later Window-Eyes was deliberately kept similar to Vocal-Eyes, which Geoffray said had proved powerful yet easy to configure (AccessWorld, 2002).",src:[VEA]});
U(DEV,"jawsdos",{ha:"Ted Henter and Bill Joyce founded Henter-Joyce in 1987 and developed JAWS, first as a DOS screen reader and later for Windows (AFB). On October 2, 1998, Henter-Joyce said JAWS for DOS 2.31 'brought the company to prominence' in 1988. The company announced it would become free to download on January 1, 1999, when sales, shipments and technical support would end, since most users had moved to Windows and JAWS for Windows. It still needed one of the hardware speech synthesizers it supported.",src:[THI,JDF]});
U(DEV,"ibmsr2",{ha:"IBM announced it on June 23, 1992 (Announcement Letter 292-363), with availability planned for September 8, 1992. It was $600, or $500 as an upgrade from Screen Reader 1.x. IBM called it the first OS/2 access product for blind users and the first to speak text from IBM's graphical user interface. It ran in all OS/2 2.0 sessions, followed the visual focus, read menus automatically, could emulate mouse point-and-click and named icons. It came with ready-made profiles and a full profiling language and compiler. It needed the IBM Screen Reader Keypad, which plugged into the mouse port on IBM PS systems or into a Screen Reader Adapter card on other computers, and a non-IBM speech synthesizer. Messages and documentation were translated into twelve languages, and installation instructions came in print, audio, online and braille.",s:{price:"$600 ($500 upgrade) in 1992"},src:[SR2A]});
U(DEV,"asap",{ha:"Developed by MicroTalk, ASAP was sold by Raised Dot Computing from 1992 (RDC Newsletter, March–April 1992). Instead of needing to be set up for each program, it decided what to speak by watching how the application reacted to keystrokes, focusing automatically on light bars, soft cursors and pop-up windows. Its features included Instant Silence, Turbo Browse (skip a little text), Quick Review from the numeric keypad, Auto Read, a user-expandable pronunciation lexicon and column detection. It needed about 30K of memory and could load high. RDC also sold an 'ASAP Classic' package with a DoubleTalk PC synthesizer modified for better performance with ASAP.",src:[RDC95]});
// Batch 26: hardware descriptions (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const VSQM="https://manualzilla.com/doc/5723857/voice-sense-qwerty-user-manual--ver-6.0-_hims",BNM="https://www.eurobraille.fr/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/bnote-Manual-3.1.0-EN.pdf",BNP="https://www.eurobraille.com/product/b-note-40/",Q40P="https://www.orbitresearch.com/products/qwerty-product/orbit-reader-q40/",Q40R="https://www.orbitresearch.com/orbit-research-expands-its-revolutionary-braille-product-family-with-the-launch-of-orbit-reader-q20-and-q40/",TRK1="https://afb.org/aw/4/4/14834",TRK2="https://afb.org/aw/5/6/14675",TLG="https://www.yumpu.com/en/document/view/21055960/the-type-lite-user-guide-freedom-scientific/11",TLS="https://portale.siva.it/en-GB/databases/products/detail/id-9061";
U(DEV,"vsq",{s:{dim:"8.6 × 4.9 × 0.7 in",os:"Windows CE 5.0",cpu:"Intel PXA270",ram:"128 MB",net:"10/100 Ethernet, Wi-Fi b/g, Bluetooth"},src:[VSQM],
 ha:"HIMS's April 2011 manual (software 6.0) lists a word processor that can write braille files by typing chords on F, D, S, J, K and L, email, a web browser, DAISY player, media player, FM radio with recording, Twitter, Google Talk and MSN Messenger. Up to seven programs can run at once.",
 desc:["The Voice Sense QWERTY is a speech-only notetaker from HIMS with a typewriter-style keyboard and no braille display. Set on a table it feels like a small, thin rectangular box, 8.6 inches long, 4.9 inches deep and 0.7 inch high, with rubber feet underneath to stop it sliding.",
 "The keyboard takes up the front part of the top and has 77 keys in six rows. The top row runs F1 to F11, then left and right square brackets, Insert and Delete, with tactile points on the frame at F1, F5 and F9. R and U, F and J, and V and M carry dots for the index fingers. Fn is at the far left of the bottom row. The arrow keys form an inverted T, with Shift and slash on either side of Up Arrow. Braille stickers are supplied for marking keys.",
 "Behind the keyboard, in the centre, is a small LCD so a sighted person can read what you type, with a speaker on each side of it.",
 "The front edge holds, from left to right: the internal microphone, a three-position key lock switch, a microphone jack, a headphone jack, a three-position audio mode switch (radio, DAISY or media), five differently shaped media buttons (previous, record, stop, play/pause, next) and a sliding power switch, pushed right for on and left for off.",
 "On the right edge, front to back, are a USB OTG port for a computer, a USB port for a memory stick and the AC adapter jack. The left edge has an SD card slot. On the back are an Ethernet port at the left and a recessed reset button at the far right. The removable battery fits a rectangular bay in the middle of the bottom, released by a latch to its left."]});
U(DEV,"bnote",{y:"2021 (manual copyright 2021–2024); second generation later",s:{cells:"20 or 40, 8-dot",dim:"235 (20) / 365 (40) × 105 × 27 mm",wt:"500 g (20) / 600 g (40)",bat:"6000 mAh, over 14 hours; 30% in 25 min",cpu:"STM32L1 + quad-core ARM Cortex-A53 1.4 GHz",ram:"512 MB",sto:"64 GB SD",net:"Wi-Fi, Bluetooth 4.2 (up to 4 devices), USB-C"},src:[BNM,BNP],
 ha:"Eurobraille sells it in standard, basic, light and basic/light versions, with or without the Perkins keyboard and Bluetooth/internal apps. Built-in functions include a file explorer, editor (docx, odt, pdf, epub, brf, txt), MP3 player, web radio and podcasts, agenda, EOLE library reader, Wikipedia access, a braille learning mode and games. A second generation adds two speakers and a USB-A port. The free b.view app shows the display to sighted companions. The 40-cell model was listed as out of stock on Eurobraille's site in October 2026.",
 desc:["The b.note is a braille display and notetaker from Eurobraille, made with 20 or 40 eight-dot cells. The 40-cell version is a long, slim bar 365 millimetres wide, 105 deep and 27 high, weighing 600 grams; the 20-cell version is 235 millimetres wide and 500 grams. A protective bumper wraps the case, and a zippered case comes with it.",
 "The braille cells run across the top. Above each cell is a routing key with two contacts: a light press moves the display forward or back, so you need not hunt for a scroll key, and a firm press routes the cursor.",
 "Eight braille keys sit in a row above the routing keys, with two thumb keys below the display, ten keys in all. On the standard and basic versions they are a Perkins-style keyboard; the light versions have none. At each end of the display is a keypad of four buttons; the up key on the left keypad opens the b.note menu.",
 "On the left edge are a small reset hole for a paper clip, the USB-C port for charging and computers, a 3.5 mm headphone jack and the key lock switch. The power button is a pull-to-activate lock: a short press puts it in transport mode and a long press of more than 2 seconds in standby. On the second generation, a USB-A port on the back takes memory sticks.",
 "Underneath, a screwed panel covers the SD card, for service only. On the second generation, two grilles at the front let out the sound of two built-in speakers."]});
U(DEV,"orq40",{yr:2024,y:"Announced March 19, 2024; shipping from June 2024",s:{cells:"40, 8-dot",price:"$1,999 (Orbit Research, 2026)",dim:"About 11 × 7 × 1 in",wt:"About 2.5 lb",kbd:"78-key QWERTY"},src:[Q40P,Q40R],
 ha:"Orbit Research announced the Q40 and 20-cell Q20 on March 19, 2024 (press release datelined Anaheim), pairing the Orbit Reader with a laptop-style keyboard; shipping was planned for June 2024. It works standalone as a book reader and note-taker, or as a display connected to up to six devices over USB and Bluetooth with JAWS, NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack and others. It translates braille in more than 70 languages and works with Orbit Teacher and Orbit Chat.",
 desc:["The Orbit Reader Q40 combines a 40-cell braille display with a full typewriter keyboard in one case. It is about 11 inches wide, 7 inches deep and a little over an inch high, and weighs about two and a half pounds. Orbit describes it as dust- and fluid-resistant.",
 "The keyboard is a full-size, laptop-style layout of 78 sculpted keys. It has a full row of twelve function keys, arrow keys in an inverted T, and Windows and Mac keys, but no separate numeric keypad.",
 "The display has 40 eight-dot cells using Orbit's TrueBraille mechanism, with a cursor routing button for each cell. A rocker key at each end of the display pans the text, and configurable thumb keys help with navigation.",
 "For connections it has a USB-C port for charging and computers, a USB-A host port for flash drives, and a full-size SD card slot.",
 "It gives audio and haptic feedback, and has a fast-charging, long-life battery."]});
U(DEV,"trekker",{yr:2003,y:"2003 (AccessWorld review, July 2003); version 2.0 reviewed 2004",s:{price:"$1,595",wt:"1.3 lb (600 g)",out:"Eloquence speech"},src:[TRK1,TRK2],
 ha:"AccessWorld reviewed the first version in July 2003. It announced streets, intersections about 30 feet ahead and points of interest from a commercial database, and could record your own landmarks, but it could not tell your direction, plan routes or change speech rate. Version 2.0 added an adjustable speech rate and route planning and following, and its case and strap hooks were sturdier. The off-the-shelf PDA kept the price down, but it gave no sign of whether the PDA was off and draining its battery.",
 desc:["The Trekker is not one box but four parts worn together on a strap around the neck: an off-the-shelf pocket PC (PDA), a GPS receiver, a speaker and a battery pack. The set weighs 1.3 pounds.",
 "The PDA is the part you work with. Static stencils came with it to use as a braille keyboard on the PDA. The on/off button sits above the screen, but it gives no sound to confirm the PDA is off.",
 "Trekker's own controls are a large oval Navigation key that works as a four-way cursor cross, with two round buttons on each side. To its right are Enter, which starts Trekker, opens menus and answers questions, and Where Am I?. To its left are Escape and the Mode button, which switches between reading and browsing.",
 "On the left side of the PDA is the Record button, which uses its built-in microphone to record your own points of interest. Trekker talks using the Eloquence synthesizer.",
 "In the box were a PDA charger and cradle, styli, the keyboard stencils and a user guide. Reviewers warned that pieces, such as the speaker cable, could come apart easily."]});
U(DEV,"typelite",{s:{cells:"40",ram:"2 MB (16 MB memory in a 2003 Italian listing)",sto:"12 MB flash, CompactFlash slot",kbd:"Laptop-style Windows keyboard",bat:"30 hours (SIVA listing)"},src:[TLG,TLS,"https://afb.org/aw/11/1/16078"],
 ha:"One of the Braille 'n Speak's spin-off siblings from Blazie Engineering, alongside the Braille Lite and Type 'n Speak (AccessWorld). Deane Blazie said the company added typewriter keyboards because many interested people didn't know braille. Its user guide describes a note-taker and personal organiser with a built-in speech synthesizer, a date book, phone book, calculator and macros. Its keyboard could stand in for a PC keyboard when linked to a computer. Italy's SIVA database listed a Type Lite 40 with bilingual Italian/English speech and 30 hours of battery.",
 desc:["The Type Lite is a notetaker from Blazie Engineering with a laptop-style typewriter keyboard and a 40-cell braille display, plus built-in speech.",
 "The keyboard is a standard portable PC layout. Its top row runs Escape, F1 to F12, then Num Lock, Insert, Delete and Break. Raised locator dots mark F1, F5, F10 and Delete.",
 "The bottom row runs, left to right: Fn, Ctrl, Windows logo key, Alt, the spacebar, Alt, Windows logo key, Application key, Ctrl, then Left Arrow, Down Arrow and Right Arrow, with Up Arrow directly above Down Arrow.",
 "The 40-cell display is below the keyboard. Between the display and the keyboard are two advance bars, and there is a rocker at each end of the display. Each cell has a cursor routing button above it, with raised locator dots to help find your place.",
 "It takes standard CompactFlash memory cards."]});
// Batch 27: Graphiti description; Brailliant BI 20X (verified Oct 31, 2026)
const GRM="https://manualzz.com/doc/73535418/orbit-research-graphiti-user-manual",B20R="https://www.helenkeller.org/a-review-of-the-brailliant-bi-20x/";
U(DEV,"graphiti",{s:{disp:"60 × 40 pins (2,400), four pin heights, touch-sensitive",io:"SD card, USB host, micro-USB, HDMI, Bluetooth",bat:"User-replaceable rechargeable"},src:[GRM],
 ha:"Orbit Research's user manual (version 1.0, October 25, 2021, with revisions back to October 2017) calls it a prototype. It can be used on its own, reading images and PDFs (first page only) from an SD card or USB drive, or connected to a PC, an HDMI source or the Orion TI-84 Plus calculator. You can draw on it by touch and save drawings as BMP files. It zooms up to 32x, rotates and inverts images, and offers several monochrome and grayscale processing variants.",
 desc:["Graphiti is a tactile graphics display from Orbit Research: a flat panel of 2,400 pins, 60 across and 40 down, each able to rise to one of four heights to form pictures you read by touch. The panel's surface is touch-sensitive, so you can also draw on it with a finger, and a slight vibration confirms many actions.",
 "Set it on a table with the keypad closest to you. The pin panel takes up the far part of the top; the keypad is in front of it, and a single status light at the left of the keypad shows red for power and green when ready.",
 "The keypad has the six braille keys in a row just below the display, dots 3, 2 and 1 on the left and 4, 5 and 6 on the right. Below them, in the middle, is a navigation pad with Up, Down, Left and Right arrows around a Select button. Nearest you is a row of three keys: a wide Space bar in the middle, Dot 7 to its left and Dot 8 to its right.",
 "On the front edge is a full-size SD card slot. On the left edge, front to back, the manual lists the on/off button, HDMI, standard USB host, micro-USB, a main power slider switch (slide it away from you to turn on) and the power jack. A note in the same manual says the power button and jack are on the right edge on some units.",
 "Small tactile dots under the sockets identify them: one dot for power, two for micro-USB, three for standard USB and four for HDMI. The battery is rechargeable and user-replaceable."]});
AD({id:"bi20x",n:"Brailliant BI 20X",co:"hw",cat:"Braille Displays",yr:2021,y:"2021 (reviewed June 2021)",o:"A 20-cell braille display and notetaker from HumanWare with an eight-dot Perkins-style keyboard, the smaller sibling of the BI 40X.",
 h:"Scott Davert reviewed it for Helen Keller Services in June 2021, after the BI 40X. It runs the same software, with an editor, PDF reading (plain text, slow to open) and Exam Mode added by firmware 1.1.1. Unlike the 40X, it has an SD card reader and a removable battery. It lacks the 40X's C keys, stereo speakers and microphone, and has half the internal memory. It uses Bluetooth 4.2 and 2.4 GHz-only Wi-Fi, against Bluetooth 5 on the 40X, and he found its Bluetooth reliable to about 20 feet versus 30 for the 40X. Price: $1,895 at review, $2,199 by January 2023.",
 s:{cells:"20, 8-dot",price:"$1,895 (2021); $2,199 (January 2023)",dim:"7.2 × 3.3 × 0.8 in",wt:"0.88 lb",sto:"16 GB, SD card, USB-A",net:"Bluetooth 4.2, 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, USB-C"},
 desc:["The Brailliant BI 20X is a small, flat braille display about 7.2 inches long, 3.3 inches deep and 0.8 inch thick, weighing just under a pound. It comes in a snug nylon case with a lanyard that closes with a magnet and leaves the ports uncovered.",
 "Hold it with the spacebars toward you. On the top, nearest you, are two spacebars. Behind them are the 20 braille cells, each with a cursor routing key behind it. Behind those is the eight-dot Perkins-style keyboard.",
 "The front edge has five buttons, left to right: the Previous key, the Left thumb key (about twice as long), a round Home key in the middle that takes you back a menu, the Right thumb key and the Next key. The two long thumb keys pan the braille display.",
 "On the left edge, front to back, are a USB-A port for thumb drives, the power button (hold for several seconds to switch on or off, tap to sleep or wake) and a USB-C port for charging and computers. The right edge has a mono speaker, two volume buttons and a 3.5 mm headphone jack.",
 "Along the back edge is a full-size SD card reader. Underneath are a braille serial number, rubber feet, and a removable battery held by two screws."],
 src:[B20R]});
U(DEV,"bi40x",{yr:2021,y:"On sale by April 2021 (Helen Keller Services review)",src:[B20R]});
